"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nTo naturally meet you in the caf\u00e9, Mr. Poirot... Harriet can't pull that off. Richard and Ada can't either. So you see, I must live to the end. [1899] But the plans that Harriet and Ada knew were not Richard's true plans. Two weeks after meeting with Harriet and Ada, Richard came to London to see me alone, at which time he told me that the question of whether Nancy should die had been weighing heavily on him. He shared my belief that Nancy had not confessed to Harriet about the conversation and that there was no other reason for her to confess at the White Hart Inn other than to clear Patrick's name from the rumors. [1900] On the other hand, Richard also understood what Harriet meant. The death of Patrick and Francis Eve was caused by the misjudgment of multiple people, and Nancy was also not blameless. [1901] When Richard admitted that he couldn't make a decision about Nancy and decided to leave it up to me, I was shocked and terrified beyond measure. He said that after Harriet, Ada, and he were dead, I could choose whether to try my best to have Nancy hanged or commit suicide and leave a completely different note. Not one saying \"May they rest in peace,\" but one revealing the truth of the death. [1902] I begged Richard not to let me make the decision alone. I kept asking him, \"Why choose me?\". [1903] \"Because, Jenny,\" he said, and I'll never forget what he said next, \"because you are the best among us. And you never let it go to your head. Yes, you've lied, but as soon as you said it, you realized you were wrong. I've believed your lies for too long, and that's unforgivable. I've fueled the fire, supporting everyone to accuse an innocent good man. Yes, he had his faults, but who among us is perfect?\" [1904] \"Well,\" I told Richard, \"I'll make the decision and fulfill your wish.\" [1905] And so, our plan was set. Now, do you want to know how we managed to mess everything up? [1906] Chapter Twenty\n[1907] How We Screwed It All Up\n[1908] \"Indeed, we'd like to know,\" said Poirot. \"Both Casterpoole and I are curious.\" [1909] \"It's all my fault,\" said Jenny's voice, hoarse. \"I'm a coward, I really fear death. Without Patrick, my life was miserable, but I got used to the pain and didn't want to end it. Any kind of life, even filled with torment, is better than death! Please don't condemn me for violating Christian teachings, but I truly don't believe in an afterlife. As the day of execution drew nearer, I grew increasingly afraid, afraid to kill. I imagined what it would be like: standing in a locked room, watching Richard drink the poison, and I didn't want to do it. But I agreed! I promised.\" [1910] \"A few months ago, the plan seemed so easy to implement, now it seems so difficult,\" said Poirot. \"Of course, you couldn't tell Richard Nielsen how much you feared it, as he held you in high regard. If you admitted your fear, he might have looked down on you. Perhaps you were also worried that he might kill you regardless of your consent.\" [1911] \"Yes! I'm really afraid he'll do that. You see, from our conversations, I realized how important it is for him that the four of us die. He once told me that if Harry and Ada couldn't be convinced, he would 'do what he had to do without their consent.' That's what he said. He's so decisive, how can I tell him I've changed my mind and don't want to die or kill?\" [1912] \"I suppose you feel guilty about it too, ma'am. You also think that this murder and death are honorable and right, don't you?\" [1913] \"Rationally, yes, I do think so,\" said Jane. \"I hope and pray that I can find the courage to complete this task.\" [1914] \"How do you plan to deal with Nancy Duane?\" I asked Jane. [1915] \"I don't know. The panic from our first meeting that night has not subsided, Mr. Poirot. I haven't made any decisions! I let Semmi continue to fabricate the story about the key and to point the finger at Nancy. I let things take their natural course and tell myself that I can always go to the authorities and save her. But... I didn't do that. Richard thinks I'm better suited to do this, but he's wrong\u2014dead wrong.\" [1916] \"I still envy Nancy because Patrick loves her, and that was the root of the disaster at Grelling's. And... I know that if I admit to conspiring to frame an innocent woman, I'll go to jail. I'm scared.\" [1917] \"Lady, tell us, what did you do? On that day... the day of the 'execution', what did you do at the Browns Hotel? \" [1918] \"I was supposed to be there at six, the time we had agreed to meet.\" [1919] \"Do you mean the four conspirators?\" [1920] \"Yes, and Semmy. All day, I watched the clock tick, waiting for that terrible moment to come. At five o'clock, I realized I couldn't do it. I couldn't! So I didn't go to the hotel. I ran through the streets of London in fear, not knowing where to go or what to do, just running. I thought Richard Nichol would come looking for me, and he would be angry because I disappointed him and everyone else. I went to 'The Happy Cafe', thinking that since I couldn't kill Richard as promised, at least I could complete this part.\" [1921] \"When I got to the cafe, I started to worry about my life. Of course, nothing happened, you saw it yourself. I thought Richard, not Nancy, would kill me. What was even more terrifying was that I believed that if he killed me, he would be doing the right thing. I deserved to die! I haven't told you a single lie, M. Poirot, please recall what I said. I was afraid to be killed? Yes, I was afraid of Richard coming to kill me. Have I done terrible things in the past? Yes. And if Richard had really caught and killed me, I believed it was only a matter of time, and I didn't want him to be punished. I know I disappointed him. Can you understand that? Richard may have been seeking death, but I wanted him to live. Although he had hurt Patrick, he was a good man.\" [1922] \"Yes, Madam.\" [1923] \"M. Poirot, that night, I wanted to tell you the truth, but I didn't have the courage.\" [1924] \"So, you believe that Richard Nigges would have found you and killed you because you didn't go to Brocks Hotel to kill him that day?\" [1925] \"Yes. I think he wouldn't have been satisfied with dying without knowing why I didn't show up at the hotel.\" [1926] \"Indeed.\" I said, seething with anger. [1927] Jane nodded. [1928] I finally understood why the scene was as it was: the three bodies were in identical positions, between the small coffee table and the chairs, upright with feet facing the door. As Mr. Poirot said, it was impossible for Harriet Spear, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nixes to all end up in that position. [1929] The similarities between the three murder scenes were suspicious, and now I understood why: the conspirators wanted the police to believe there was only one killer. On the same night, at the same hotel, three bodies, with a cufflink in each mouth. Any competent detective could have deduced this was the work of one person, and an obsessive one at that. But they knew there were more than one killer, so they were afraid, afraid of the truth being revealed, so they went to great lengths to make the three murder scenes look very similar, even overdoing it. [1930] The bodies lying neatly on the ground, upright and identical, also aligned with their intentions: what happened at Browns Hotel wasn't a murder, but an execution. After each execution, there were subsequent procedures, like a religious ritual. I thought it was better to arrange the bodies properly than to leave them lying around, not looking like the work of an ordinary killer or a garden killer. [1931] Suddenly, a younger Jane Hobbs appeared in my mind, tidying up the beds in the rooms at Jesus College, Cambridge, one by one. She would arrange each bed in the same pattern...\n[1932] Beds, death beds...\n[1933] Pattern, disrupted pattern...\n[1934] \"Richard Naylegh committed suicide.\" I blurted out involuntarily, \"It was suicide. He wanted it to look like murder, so he followed the pattern of the other two, he wanted us to think it was the same killer, but he had to lock the door from the inside. That's why he hid the key behind the tiles in the fireplace, pretending that the killer took the key and then escaped through the window. If the key was found, we would have been suspicious - and we were indeed puzzled - why the killer locked the door from the inside and hid the key in the room, yet escaped through the window. But we would still believe there was only one killer. Naylegh only cared about that. But if the window was closed and the key was found, we would only conclude that Richard Naylegh committed suicide. He would not take the risk of letting us draw that conclusion, right? Once we came to that conclusion, the plot to frame Nancy Duane for the murders of three people would fail, and we would be more likely to believe that Naylegh first killed Harriet Spear and Ada Gransbury and then committed suicide.\" [1935] \"Yes,\" Jane said, \"I think you're right.\" [1936] \"The position of the cufflinks is different...\" murmured Poirot, raising his eyebrows at me to continue. [1937] \"The cufflinks were so close to Nigges' throat because he convulsed after taking the poison, causing his mouth to open. He had to be careful to swallow the poison while keeping his body straight, placing the cufflinks between his lips, but they slipped into his mouth. Unlike Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry, Richard Nigges died without anyone helping him arrange the scene, so the cufflinks couldn't be placed in the same position as planned.\" [1938] \"Do you think Mr. Nigges would have taken the poison, not knowing why you weren't at the hotel?\" Poirot asked Jenny. [1939] \"I never believed it until I read the news of his death in the paper.\" [1940] \"Ah.\" Poirot's expression was inscrutable. [1941] \"For a long time, Richard had been looking forward to ending his life on Thursday night, putting an end to years of guilt and torment.\" Mrs. Vane said, \"I believe he went to the Blaxland Hotel to die, so even if I hadn't shown up to kill him, he had completed his self-inflicted death.\" [1942] \"Thank you, madam.\" Poirot stood up, almost losing his balance due to sitting for too long. [1943] \"What will you do with me, Mr. Poirot?\" [1944] \"Please stay at home until I or Mr. Cattermole visit again. If you run away again, it will be even worse for you.\" [1945] \"Even if I stay here, things won't get better.\" Mrs. Vane said, her eyes filled with despair and emptiness. \"Yes, Mr. Cattermole, you shouldn't worry about me. I'm prepared for what's to come.\" [1946] Without a doubt, she said this to reassure me, but it made me feel fear. Her attitude seemed to see the future, to see how terrible things would happen. But whatever happens, I know I'm not ready and don't want to be ready. [1947] Chapter 21\n[1948] All demons\n[1949] All day long, Poirot didn't speak, except for repeating twice that we must go to Grelling immediately and not waste any time. He looked preoccupied and obviously didn't want to talk. [1950] We returned to our residence and saw Stanley Bill waiting for us. Poirot asked, \"What's the matter? You've come because of my artwork, haven't you?\" [1951] \"What, sir? Oh, your artwork? No, that's fine, sir. Actually\u2026\" Bill took an envelope out of his pocket and handed it to Poirot, \"This has the answer you wanted.\" [1952] \"Thank you, officer. But there must be something, right? You look anxious. Is it about that?\" [1953] \"Yes, sir. I came to deliver a message. A man named Ambrose Flavard went to Scotland Yard to see Mr. Cargraves. He said he's the doctor from Gregholing village and needs to see him urgently, saying he can help.\" [1954] Poirot looked at me and then back at Stanley Bill, saying, \"We were just about to go there. Do you know why he wants to see Cargraves?\" [1955] \"I'm afraid I do, sir. It's not good news, sir. A woman named Margaret Ernst was attacked and is dying\u2026\"\n[1956] \"Oh, no.\" I muttered. [1957] \"And she wants to see Mr. Cader before she dies. Sir, considering what Dr. Flavard said, I suggest you hurry. The car is outside and can take you to the station.\" [1958] Considering Poirot's methodical and dislike of chaos, I said, \"Can we give us half an hour to prepare?\" [1959] Bill looked at his watch and said again, \"Sir, we have at most five to ten minutes, we can't waste time, otherwise, you'll miss the next train.\" [1960] I had to admit, this time Poirot was faster than me, he had already packed his luggage and was hurrying me downstairs, saying, \"Hurry up, my friend.\" [1961] In the car, I decided not to remain silent, even though I knew Poirot didn't want to talk. \"If I didn't go to that damn village, Margaret Ernst wouldn't have been attacked.\" I said coldly, \"Someone must have seen me go to her house and stay there for a long time.\" [1962] \"Long enough for her to tell you everything, or almost everything. But since she has already told the police everything, what's the point of killing her?\" [1963] \"Revenge, punishment. Honestly, it doesn't make sense. If Nancy Duane is innocent, then Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid are the masterminds behind it - I mean the living masterminds, then why would Jenny and Kid kill Margaret Ernst? Margaret's words won't threaten them, and she never harmed Patrick and Francis Ives.\" [1964] \"I agree with your point of view. I also don't think Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid want to kill Margaret Ernst.\" [1965] The rain pattered on the car window, making it difficult for us to hear each other and concentrate. I asked again, \"Who could it be? I thought all the problems were solved -\"\n[1966] \"Carter, you can't really believe that, can you?\" [1967] \"Yes, I do. I guess you'll say I'm wrong, but didn't all the problems get solved? Before we knew Margaret Ernst was attacked, everything was clear.\" [1968] \"You actually think everything is clear!\" Poirot looked out the window at the rain pounding against the glass, chuckling foolishly. [1969] \"Well, to me, it's quite clear. All the killers are dead. Edith killed Harry with her consent, and Richard Nix killed her willingly. Since Jeanne didn't kill Nix as planned, he chose to commit suicide. Jeanne Hobbs and Samuel Kid didn't kill anyone. Of course, they were involved in the murder, but I think it's not really a murder, but \u2013\"\n[1970] \"Voluntarily sentenced to death?\" [1971] \"Exactly.\" [1972] \"What an ingenious plan, isn't it? Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, Richard Nigges, and Jenny Hobbs, let's call them A, B, C, and D, and see how clever their plan was.\" [1973] \"Why not use their names?\" I asked. [1974] Poirot didn't answer me. \"A, B, C, and D, all four had guilt and sought redemption for their souls. They all agreed that they would use their lives to make up for the sins they had committed, so they planned to kill each other: B killed A, then C killed B, and finally D killed C.\"\n[1975] \"But D didn't kill C, did she? D represents Jenny Hobbs, who didn't kill Richard Nigges.\" [1976] \"Perhaps, but the plan was for her to do so. Moreover, D must live long enough to see E \u2013 Nancy Drew, who was hanged for murdering A, B, and C. By then, D\u2026\" He suddenly stopped, said \"D\" again, and then said, \"Demise, that's the correct answer.\" [1977] \"What?\" [1978] \"Your crossword puzzle. A six-letter word meaning 'death', do you remember? I said the answer was 'murder', you said no, unless 'murder' began with\u2026\" He stopped again, shaking his head. [1979] \"If 'murder' had started with D, that would have been perfect. Yes, I remember. Poirot, are you all right? His eyes glinted with strange green light, which occasionally occurred. [1980] \"What? Of course! If 'murder' had started with D, that would have been perfect! Yes! That's it! My friend, you've done me a great favor! Now I believe\u2026yes, that's the truth. The truth must be like this. The old couple \u2013 ah, everything is clear now!\" [1981] \"Please explain what's going on.\" [1982] \"Okay, okay, but I need to prepare myself first.\" [1983] \"What's not ready? What are you waiting for?\" [1984] \"Kattakur, you at least need to give me twenty seconds to collect my thoughts and organize my language. I need time to explain to someone who's completely in the dark, and I see you haven't understood anything yet. You say everything is clear, but the story Jenny Hobbs told us this morning was a carefully crafted lie! Don't you see that?\" [1985] \"Eh... Do you mean... \"\n[1986] \"Harriet Spear wants to pin three murders on Nancy Drew, making her hang, but would Richard Nixes really agree to that? And would he leave Nancy's fate in the hands of Jenny Hobbs? This leader, Richard Nixes, a beloved authority figure who has been guilty for sixteen years for unjustly attacking Patrick Eves, would he do that?\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHowever, while working with Nancy, Jenny pretended to be involved in another plot, making Richard Nix believe she was his accomplice.\" [2382] \"That doesn't sound 'simple' to me.\" I said, \"It's quite complex.\" [2383] \"No, no, friend, it's not complicated at all. Your problem is that you've mixed different versions of this story together. You have to forget everything Jenny said at Samuel Kid's house and erase it from your mind completely. She was lying from beginning to end, of course, I don't deny there was some truth in it. The most convincing lies have a grain of truth. Soon, Jenny will tell us the whole truth, she's not afraid of anything now. But first, my friend, I must commend you, it was your clue at the Saint's Churchyard that made me understand everything.\" [2384] Poirot turned to Jenny and said, \"You told Harriet Westphal that Patrick Yves was helping parishioners communicate with their deceased loved ones for money, and that Nancy Duane often went to the vicar's house at night to talk to her deceased husband William. How many times have I heard this terrible, wicked rumor? Many, many times. Miss Hobbs, you admitted to us that day that you said these lies out of jealousy and weakness. But that's not true!\" [2385] \"Standing in front of the desecrated grave of Patrick and Francis Yves, Kitcher told me, 'If Jenny Hobbs made up lies not to hurt Patrick Yves, but to help him?' Kitcher realized a key point that I took for granted. This has never been suspected, so I never verified it: that Harriet Westphal was crazy about her late husband George. Didn't Poirot know how much Harriet loved George? Didn't he also know how George's death turned Harriet from a happy, kind woman into a bitter, evil demon? It's hard for an outsider to imagine such a terrible, destructive disaster that could wipe out all joy and destroy all virtues. Yes, of course, I know Harriet Westphal has experienced such a disaster. I believe it, which made me not doubt! [2386] \"I also know that Jenny Hobbs loves Patrick Yves and is willing to sacrifice her own happiness and abandon her fianc\u00e9 Samuel Kid to continue serving the Yves couple. It's a selfless love, willing to pay without expecting anything in return. But in the story Jenny and Nancy told us, they said that Jenny lied out of jealousy, jealous of the love Patrick gave to Nancy. That's not possible! It's inconsistent! We must consider both feasibility and psychological factors. Patrick Yves married Francis without punishment from Jenny, accepting the reality that he belonged to another woman. She continued to be his faithful servant in the vicarage, helping him and his wife, and the couple treated her well. For years, Jenny had selflessly loved and served Patrick, so why did his love for Nancy suddenly drive her to lie and cause a series of events that ultimately destroyed him? The answer is, such a thing is impossible.\" [2387] \"It's not jealousy or longing that drove Jenny Hobbs to lie. It's a completely different reason. Miss Hobbs, you've been helping the man you love, haven't you? You could say, to save him. When my clever friend Kitteral said his theory, I realized what the truth was. It's so obvious, Poirot was so dull that he didn't see it!\" [2388] Jenny looked at me and asked, \"What theory?\" [2389] I opened my mouth to explain, but Poirot interrupted, \"When Harriet Cypers told you she saw Nancy Duane visiting the vicarage at night, you immediately sensed danger. You knew about their rendezvous \u2013 you lived in the vicarage, how could you not know? You were eager to protect Patrick Yves' good name. How could you protect his reputation? How could you explain Nancy Duane's late-night visits when Francis Yves was not at home? How could you fool the gossips? Just when you were about to give up hope, it seemed like magic, you suddenly thought of a way. You decided to use temptation and fantasy to eliminate Harriet's threat.\" [2390] Jenny said nothing, staring blankly into the distance. [2391] \"Miss Hariot Westcliff and Nancy Duane have one thing in common,\" Poirot continued. \"Their husbands died young. You told Miss Hariot Westcliff that Patrick Eves could help Nancy communicate with her late husband, William Duane, of course for a fee. Naturally, such a thing had to be kept secret and not revealed to the church or anyone in the village. But you hinted to Miss Hariot Westcliff that if she wanted, Patrick could also help her, just like Nancy, communicate with her husband's spirit. And she and George might\u2026 well, even if they couldn't be together again, at least they could communicate in some way. Tell me, what was her reaction when you told her this?\" [2392] There was a long silence before Jenny said, \"She was salivating, eager to communicate with her late husband. She said she would pay any amount of money to talk to George again. Mr. Poirot, you can't imagine how much she loved that man. I watched her face\u2026 it was like a dead woman coming back to life. I tried to explain the whole thing to Patrick, to tell him what had happened, but I had solved it. You see, I made a promise to Miss Hariot Westcliff without his consent. I knew Patrick wouldn't agree to this, but I had no other choice! I didn't want him to stop me. Can you understand that?\" [2393] \"I understand, madam.\" [2394] \"I hope I can convince him. He is a man of principles, but I know he wants to protect Frances and doesn't want this scandal to hurt her, and he also wants to protect Nancy. And this is the only way to keep Miss Hariot Westcliff silent, the only way! Patrick only had to occasionally comfort Miss Hariot Westcliff with some words, pretending they were from George Westcliff. And he didn't need to ask her for money. I told him all this, but he wouldn't listen. He found it terrifying.\" [2395] \"He would naturally feel that way.\" Poirot said softly. \"Please continue.\" [2396] \"He said that doing as I suggested would be unfair and immoral to Hattie, and would make him more likely to be destroyed. I begged him to reconsider. What harm could there be in making Hattie happy? But Patrick was firm, insisting that I tell Hattie that he couldn't do it. His instructions were clear: 'Jane, don't tell her you lied, or she'll suspect the whole thing is untrue.' He wanted me to tell Hattie that her wishes couldn't be fulfilled.\" [2397] \"And you had to tell her that, with no other choice,\" I said. [2398] \"There was truly no other choice. I told Hattie that Patrick had refused her request, and she immediately turned against him and spread my lies throughout the village. Actually, Patrick could have ruined her reputation, saying she had always wanted him to provide unethical religious services and that if he refused, she would defame him for desecrating God and violating doctrine. But he didn't do that. He said that no matter how maliciously Hattie attacked him, he wouldn't defame her reputation. How foolish! He could have made her shut up immediately, but his morals were too high, and he didn't do that!\" [2399] \"Was it at this point that you went to seek Nancy Drew's advice?\" [2400] \"Yes. I felt it shouldn't only be me and Patrick who were troubled. Nancy was also involved in this. I asked her if I should publicly admit that I had lied, but she advised me not to. She said, 'Jane, I'm worried that doing so will bring trouble to Patrick and me. Jane, the wisest thing to do is to retreat into the background, say nothing, and not sacrifice yourself. I'm not sure if you're strong enough to withstand Hattie's defamation.' She underestimated me. I was very unsettled at the time, and perhaps I looked on the verge of collapse, as I was too worried about Patrick and worried that Hattie would ruin him. But Mr. Poirot, I am not a weak person.\" [2401] \"I know you're not afraid.\" [2402] \"I'm not afraid. The news of Harry Sipper's death gave me a new sense of purpose. The killer did the world a favor by getting rid of that despicable hypocrite.\" [2403] \"Your words bring us back to the question: who killed Harry Sipper? You told us it was Edith Gransby, but that was a lie.\" [2404] \"I don't need to tell you the truth, Poirot sir, since you know it as well as I do.\" [2405] \"Then please have some pity on Mr. Cattermole, who still doesn't understand the whole situation.\" [2406] \"It would be best if you tell him, yes?\" Jane smiled, a weary smile. Suddenly, she seemed more ethereal to me, as if she might vanish at any moment. [2407] \"Very well,\" said Poirot. \"I'll start with Harriet Sipper and Edith Gransberry, two stubborn women who thought themselves just, pushing a good man to an early grave. Did they feel remorse for the deceased? No, on the contrary, they opposed burying him in a consecrated grave. After Richard Nigel's patient persuasion, did these two women regret treating Patrick Ives so badly? Of course not, they wouldn't. It was then, Jane, that I knew you were lying. The truth lies in your story.\" [2408] Jane shrugged and said, \"Anything is possible.\" [2409] \"No, only the truth is possible. I know that Harriet Sipper and Edith Gransberry would never willingly take their own lives. Therefore, they were killed by someone else. To say they committed suicide is easy! You think that if Poirot knew all the dead were willingly taking their own lives, his gray brain cells wouldn't work. It was their chance to repent! What an extraordinary, imaginative story, one that people would believe without question, who would suspect it was fabricated?\" [2410] \"It's my safety measure, to be used only when necessary,\" said Jane. \"I hope you never find me, but I'm afraid you will.\" [2411] \"If I find you, you will need an alibi from seven fifteen to eight ten to protect yourself, and Nancy Drew as well. You and Samuel Kid would only be accused of framing an innocent woman, but not of murder or conspiracy to murder. That's a clever move, admitting to doing wrong to avoid a more serious charge. You eliminated your enemies, and no one would suffer for it, as long as we believed your story \u2013 that Edith Garnsey killed Harriet Spear, Richard Nigger killed Edith Garnsey, and then committed suicide. Lady, your plan was brilliant, but it still outsmarts Hercule Poirot!\" [2412] \"Richard wanted to die.\" Jane said angrily, \"He was not killed, he was seeking death.\" [2413] \"Yes.\" Poirot said, \"That is the only truth in this whole lie.\" [2414] \"It was all his fault. I would not have killed anyone if it weren't for Richard.\" [2415] \"But you did kill, and not just once. It was Cacophony's careless remark that led me to the clue.\" [2416] \"What did he say?\" asked Jane. [2417] \"He said, if the murder started with D\u2026\"\n[2418] Hearing Poirot thank me made me feel a little uneasy. I didn't understand why those few casual words were so important. [2419] Poirot began to speak again: \"Lady, after hearing your story and leaving Samuel Kidd's house, we naturally discussed what you said: the plan you made with Richard Nigges\u2026 Allow me to say that this plan is quite attractive. Simple and clean, like a line of dominoes. But after thinking about it again, I realized that the order of events had changed. It wasn't D down, then C, then B, and finally A; it was B who knocked A down, and then C knocked B down\u2026 But that's not the point.\" [2420] What was he talking about? Jane seemed to have understood it even less. [2421] \"Ah, I must explain it more clearly,\" Poirot said. \"Lady, to make it easier to imagine the order of the murder, I used letters to replace the names. In the story you told us at Samuel Kid's house, the plan was that B killed A, then C killed B, then D killed C, and then D waited for E to be condemned and hanged for murdering A, B, and C, and finally D committed suicide. That is, in your story, you are the D. Understand, Miss Hobbs?\" [2422] Jenny nodded. [2423] \"Good. Mr. Cattermole is a crossword puzzle enthusiast, and at that moment, his interests coincided with mine, and he asked me to think of a word with six letters that meant 'death'. I said 'murder'. Mr. Cattermole said it was not. He said, 'If \"murder\" starts with D, it fits in.' Later, I remembered his words and dared to guess what would happen if the murder really started with D? If the first murderer was not Adela Gransbury, but you, Miss Hobbs?\" [2424] \"As time went on, I came to the conclusion that this was the case. I also knew why you definitely killed Harriet Sipper. From Grilling to the Bluxton Hotel, Harriet and Adela neither took the same train nor the same car, so they didn't know each other's existence, which means there was no such a plan of one killing the other. It was all lies.\" [2425] \"What is the truth?\" I asked eagerly. [2426] \"Harriet Sipper and Edith Garnet both thought they were going to London alone for personal reasons. Harriet was called by Jane, who said she urgently needed to see her and asked for absolute secrecy. Jane told Harriet that she had booked a room for her at the Bluxton Hotel and that she would meet her between 3:30 and 4:00 pm on Thursday to discuss important matters. Harriet accepted Jane's invitation because the reasons mentioned in the invitation letter were irresistible. [2427] \"You offered her something that Patrick Ivor had refused to give her years ago, didn't you, Miss? Communication with her late husband. You told her that her husband George Sipper wanted to communicate with her through you, that he had tried sixteen years ago but failed. Now, George wants to send a message to his beloved wife through you, the medium. He has spoken to you from the other side! Oh, I believe you made a convincing case! Harriet could not resist the temptation and would believe you because she desperately wanted it to be true. She believed the lie you told her years ago that spirits could communicate with the living people they loved, and she never doubted it. [2428] \"You are a clever old man, M. Poirot,\" said Jane. \"Full marks!\" [2429] \"Kitchener, tell me, do you now understand who the old woman and the young man who could be her son in room 317 were talking about? You were always confused about who Southy and Samuel Kid were talking about in room 317.\" [2430] \"I can't say I understand. Oh, I still don't understand.\" [2431] \"Let's review what Rafael Bockarov said. He heard South Nancy Dunn impersonating Harriet Westbury say, 'He no longer trusts her. He doesn't like her now. It was her choice to go. She's old enough to be his mother now.' Think about the phrase 'He doesn't like her now.' That's the premise, and then comes the two reasons for the 'not liking.' One of them is that she's old enough to be his mother now. No, 'Now, she's old enough to be his mother.' Don't you understand, Carter? If she's old enough to be his mother now, she should have always been old enough to be his mother. There's no other possibility!\" [2432] \"Isn't it just a long sentence?\" I said. \"I mean, the sentence can still make sense without the word 'now.' 'He doesn't like her. It was her choice to go. She's old enough to be his mother.'\" [2433] \"But, my friend, that would be absurd!\" Poirot exclaimed. \"It's illogical. The sentence has the word 'now,' and we can't pretend it's not there, ignore the word 'now,' it's right there in our ears!\" [2434] \"I'm afraid I can't agree with your point of view,\" I said uneasily. \"If I must try to think about it, I would say that the sentence is trying to convey that before she left, the young man didn't particularly care or notice the age difference between them, perhaps not so obvious. Now that she's no longer youthful, he has moved on and fallen in love with a younger, more attractive woman and trusts her\u2014\"\n[2435] Poirot couldn't help but interrupt my words, his face turning red with impatience. \"In my opinion, your guess is absurd, Carter! Listen to me! Listen to the original sentence again, the order of the words is like this, 'He doesn't like her now, it was her choice to go, she's old enough to be his mother now!' First, he doesn't like her, then comes the second reason! The sentence structure is clear, both of these unfortunate reasons are 'now' situations, not the past.\" [2436] \"You don't have to yell at me, Poirot. I understand your point, but I still can't agree. Not everyone is as concerned with word choice as you are. My interpretation must be correct, yours is wrong, just as you said, there's no other possibility. You just said it yourself, if she's old enough to be his mother now, then she should have always been old enough to be his mother.\" [2437] \"Ketsepalu, Ketsepalu, I'm starting to lose hope in you! Think about what comes after this sentence. Raffles Burbank heard Samuel Kid, pretending to be Richard Nix, say, 'I don't agree with what you just said about being old enough to be his mother, I don't agree at all.' In response, Nancy Drew, pretending to be Harriet, said, 'Well, neither of us can prove we're right, so let's agree to disagree.' Why can't they prove themselves right? Is a woman old enough to be a man's mother not a simple biological fact? If she's only four years older than him, of course, she's not old. Is that not obvious? If she's twenty years older, then she's old enough to be his mother. That's also indisputable.\" [2438] \"What if she's thirteen years older?\" Jean Hobbs closed her eyes and said, \"Or twelve? There are always exceptions... Of course, I'm not talking about this particular case.\" [2439] It seems that Jean understands Poirot's point, but I don't. [2440] \"Thirteen years, twelve years, it doesn't matter!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHercule Poirot rarely makes mistakes, but occasionally they happen, and this must be the case, because room 238 was indeed very tidy, without a trace of disorder, just as tidy as Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry's rooms. [329] \"I have to close the window and see if there's anything different.\" Poirot muttered to himself. After closing the window, he re-examined the room, still feeling uneasy. He just didn't like room 238, even if he came to the Brouks Hotel and went straight to this room, he would still feel uncomfortable. [330] Suddenly, a question popped into his mind, interrupting his thoughts. The fireplace! There was a tile that didn't align properly on the fireplace, it was uneven and protruded a little, a bit loose. There was a loose tile in the room, and Poirot wouldn't be able to sleep. Poirot stared at Richard Nigel's corpse and said to it, \"If I were in this position, no, there's no if. [331] As soon as Poirot touched it, the tile came off with a thud, and something else fell with it: a key with \"238\" written on it. \"My God,\" Poirot muttered, \"it seems the search was not thorough enough.\" [332] Poirot put the key back in place and began to examine other parts of the room, inch by inch, but found nothing. He then went to room 317 and finally room 121. I finished my task and was preparing to report the exciting new situation to him when he was still there. [333] Poirot was still the same, insisting on saying his discovery first, how he discovered the key. I can only say that in Belgium, he did not seem too arrogant. He said proudly, \"Understand my meaning, my friend? The window was not opened by Richard Nigel, but was opened after his death! The killer locked the door of room 238 and needed to find a way to escape. So he hid the key behind a loose tile and then climbed down outside the window. It's also possible that he loosened that tile. [334] \"Why didn't the killer carry the key with him and leave the room in a normal way?\" I asked. [335] \"Why didn't the killer carry the key with him and leave the room in a normal way?\" I asked. [336] \"I've been asking myself that question, but so far, I haven't found an answer,\" Poirot said. \"I have found out that there are no keys hidden in this room or Room 317. The murderer must have taken the two keys with him when he left the Blaukopf Hotel, but why didn't he take the third one? Why did he treat Richard Nigges differently?\" [337] \"I don't have any good ideas,\" I said. \"Listen, I've talked to John Good, the employee -\"\n[338] \"The most reliable employee,\" Poirot corrected me with a wink. [339] \"Yes, indeed. Well... whether he's reliable or not, the information he provided has been very helpful. You're right, the three victims were related. I found the addresses of the deceased: Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransberry both lived in a place called Gleeholm in the Pigeon Valley.\" [340] \"Good. What about Richard Nigges?\" [341] \"He lives in Bideford, Devon. But he's connected too; he booked all three rooms\u2014Edith's, Harriet's, and his own, and paid the deposits in advance.\" [342] \"Really? That's interesting\u2026\" Poirot muttered, stroking his mustache. [343] \"My feeling is very strange,\" I said. \"The biggest mystery is: if Harriet Westpall and Edith Gransbury were supposed to leave from the same village on the same day, why didn't they come together? Why didn't they come to the hotel together? I asked John Good about this several times, and he insisted that Harriet arrived two hours before Edith on Wednesday, exactly two hours.\" [344] \"What about Richard Neville?\" [345] I decided to clarify any information related to Neville first so that I wouldn't have to keep answering Poirot's \"What about Richard Neville?\" questions. [346] \"He arrived an hour earlier than Harriet Sipper, the earliest of the three, but it was not John Good who received him. It was a young employee, Thomas Brineau. I also found out that all three victims came to London by train, none of them drove. Do you want to know these details, but \u2014\"\n[347] \"I need to know everything,\" Poirot said. [348] It was clear that he wanted to take charge and investigate the case himself, which both annoyed and reassured me. \"Braunsan Hotel has several cars that go to the station to pick people up,\" I told Poirot. \"The fees are high, but they are willing to wait for you. Three weeks ago, Richard Nigges booked three cars through John Good to pick him up, Harriet Sipper, and Ada Gransbury separately. And all the fees, rooms, and car rentals were paid in advance by Nigges.\" [349] \"I want to know if he is wealthy,\" Poirot said thoughtfully. \"There are too many cases of people being killed for money. What do you think, Captain Calver, do we have more clues now?\" [350] \"Hmm...,\" I said, deciding to be more proactive since he asked. In Poirot's dictionary, imagining the possibilities of the case was a good thing, so I decided to summarize a theory based on the facts. \"Richard Nigges booked the rooms and paid the rent, which means he knew the other two would come. However, Harriet Sipper probably didn't know that Ada Gransbury was also staying at Braunsan Hotel, and Ada Gransbury didn't know that Harriet Sipper would come.\" [351] \"Yes, that's possible.\" [352] Encouraged by Poirot, I continued: \"Perhaps the key condition for the killer was that Ada and Harriet didn't know each other was coming to the Brooks Hotel. If that's the case, and Richard Nigel knew both ladies would be staying at the Brooks\u2026\"\n[353] Poirot took over: \"My friend, our thoughts are on the same track. Is it possible that Richard Nigel didn't know he was an accomplice to the killer? The killer may have found a reason to convince Richard Nigel to bring the other two victims to the Brooks Hotel. The question is: is it important that Ada and Harriet didn't know each other would be staying at the same hotel? Is it important for Richard Nigel, the killer, or both?\" [354] \"Maybe Richard Nigel had a plan, and the killer had another plan?\" [355] \"Very likely,\" Poirot said. \"Our next step is to gather as much information as possible about Harriet Spear, Richard Nigel, and Ada Gransbury. Who were they in life? What were their desires, grievances, and secrets? We need to go to the village of Glebeholme to find the answers. Perhaps we can find Jan and the mysterious PIJ there.\" [356] \"I have checked, and there is no guest named Jenny in the hotel. There hasn't been since last night.\" [357] \"Yes, I don't think she's here. The waitress named Phyllis Spurling told me that Jenny lives on the other side of the city. This means she lives in London, not Devonshire, not in Pebble Mill. If Jenny really lives on the 'other side of the city,' she wouldn't need to book a room at Brocks Han Hotel.\" [358] \"By the way, Henry Nigges, Richard's brother, is on his way here from Devonshire. Richard Nigges and his family live together. Moreover, I have arranged a team to investigate the hotel's guests.\" [359] \"Your efficiency is always commendable, Cattupall,\" said Poirot, patting my arm. [360] I felt the need to admit to Poirot that I hadn't done something well. \"The delivery of the room's dinner is a bit of a mystery. I haven't been able to find out who placed the order or who delivered it.\" [361] \"Don't worry,\" Pollock said. \"I will clarify it when we gather at the restaurant later. Now, let's take a walk in the hotel garden. Sometimes, just a casual look can lead to new ideas.\" [362] As soon as we came out, Pollock complained about the weather, and it was indeed getting cloudy. So, I suggested, \"Shall we go back?\" [363] \"No, no, don't go back yet. Changing the environment is good for those little gray cells. Maybe the trees can block the wind. I don't mind the cold, but there's a difference between comfortable and uncomfortable cold, and today's is the latter.\" [364] As soon as we entered the entrance of the Baskerville Hotel garden, we stopped. When I looked at the endless yellow-green grass and the elegant shrubs at the end, I thought that Luca Lazari was not exaggerating. The garden was really beautiful. I saw such an artistic garden for the first time in London. It was not only a transformation of nature, but also a conquest, and it was still pleasing to the eye even in the cold wind. [365] \"What do you think?\" I asked Pollock. \"Shall we go in?\" I thought it would be very pleasant to take a walk on the winding but straight path among the trees. [366] \"I don't know,\" Poirot frowned. \"This weather...\u201d He shivered. [367] \"\u2026It will be colder in the garden, undoubtedly,\" I impatiently finished his sentence. \"But we only have two places to go, Poirot. Either inside the hotel or outside, where do you want to go?\" [368] \"I have a better idea!\" he exclaimed to me. \"Let's take the bus out!\" [369] \"Take the bus? Where to?\" [370] \"Anywhere! It doesn't matter. We'll change buses right away, so we can change environments without freezing. Let's go. We can look out the window and see the city's scenery. Who knows what we might discover.\" He set off decisively. [371] I shook my head as I followed him. \"You're thinking about Jean, aren't you?\" I said, \"We won't see her\u2014\"\n[372] \"It's better than standing here staring at branches and grass!\" Poirot snapped. [373] Ten minutes later, we were on a bus. The windows were covered in fog, and we couldn't see anything, even after wiping them with a handkerchief. [374] I tried to reason with Poirot, \"Speaking of Jean\u2026\"\n[375] \"Hmm?\" [376] \"She might be in danger, but she has nothing to do with the Brownswood Hotel. There's no evidence of any connection. None at all.\" [377] \"I disagree with your assessment, my friend,\" Poirot said sadly. \"I'm becoming more convinced there's a connection.\" [378] \"Really? Why, for heaven's sake, Poirot?\" [379] \"Because there are two peculiar similarities between them.\" [380] \"What are they?\" [381] \"You'll find out, Caderpillar. Really, if you open your mind and think carefully about what you've learned so far, you'll find out.\" [382] Behind us sat an elderly mother and her middle-aged daughter, discussing the difference between ordinary pastry and excellent pastry. [383] \"Caderpillar, did you hear what they said?\" Poirot whispered to me, \"Difference. We can't focus on the similarities; we should focus on the differences \u2013 that's what will guide us to the killer.\" [384] \"What kind of difference?\" I asked Poirot. [385] \"For example, the difference between the third case and the other two. Why were there so many differences in the scene of Richard Nigges' case? Why did the killer lock the door from the inside instead of from the outside? Why did he hide the key behind a loose fireplace tile instead of taking it with him? Why did he climb down from a tree next to the window to leave in that way, instead of walking normally down the corridor? At first, I suspected he might have heard someone talking in the corridor and been afraid to leave Richard Nigges' room without being seen.\" [386] \"That sounds reasonable,\" I said. [387] \"No, I don't think that's the reason.\" [388] \"Oh, why not?\" [389] \"Because the position of the cufflink in Richard Nigges's mouth was different from the other two: completely in the mouth, almost to the throat, not between the lips.\" [390] I complained, \"Here we go again. I really don't think -\"\n[391] \"Ah! Wait, Ketteridge, let's see\u2026\"\n[392] The car stopped, and Poirot stretched his neck, looking at each passenger as they boarded. When the last passenger\u2014a tall, slender man with a suit of tweed and more hair on his ears than on his head\u2014also boarded, Poirot sighed in resignation. [393] \"Because you're disappointed that there's no Jane?\" I said. I had to say it to convince him. [394] \"No, my friend. I am disappointed, but not for the reasons you think. Every time I think of it, I feel disappointed, as it is almost impossible for me to see Jane again in this large city of London. But I remain hopeful.\" [395] \"You always say to follow science, is this not a bit unrealistic now?\" [396] \"Do you think hope is the enemy of science, rather than the driving force? I don't agree with your view, just as I don't agree with your view of the cufflinks. The case of Richard Nigges and the cases of the two other ladies are very different. The position of the cufflinks cannot be explained by the killer hearing someone in the corridor and wanting to avoid them.\" [397] \"Most cases get to this point - of course, it refers to the cases that Heracles Poirot intervenes in - someone doesn't want to participate in the conversation, but wants to think quietly to himself, thinking it's more comfortable, but it's actually ineffective.\" [398] In my mind, facing a reasonable and excellent listener, my silence expresses the following point of view: the slight difference in the position of Richard Nigges' cufflinks is not a critical issue at all. The mouth is the mouth, and any part of the mouth belongs to the mouth. In the killer's eyes, he did the same thing to the three victims: opened their mouths and put a gold cufflink with a combination of letters inside. [399] As for why the killer hid the key behind a loose brick in the fireplace, I haven't come up with a reasonable explanation yet. For the killer, it would be faster and more convenient to take the key away or wipe the fingerprints and throw it on the carpet. [400] The mother and daughter behind us had finished talking about the pastries and started talking about butter. [401] \"We should go back to the hotel,\" Polo said. [402] \"But we just got on the bus!\" I objected. [403] \"Yes, that's right. We can't be too far from the Blaux Han Hotel, as we'll have to go to the restaurant later.\" [404] I slowly exhaled, knowing that there was no need to ask him why in this situation. It was he who came up with the idea in the first place, and now it was he who wanted to go back. [405] \"We have to get off this bus and transfer to another one to go back,\" he said. \"Perhaps the scenery on the next bus will be more beautiful.\" [406] It turned out to be true. Poirot still hadn't seen any sign of Miss Carnaby, which made him anxious, while I enjoyed the interesting scenery and realized once again why I loved London so much. A man dressed as a clown was performing magic tricks, badly, which I had never seen before, but people kept throwing coins into his hat; the most remarkable thing was a dog that looked exactly like a famous politician, and a homeless man sitting on the ground with an open suitcase beside him, eating things out of it as if it were his personal food cart. \"Poirot, look,\" I said. \"That guy doesn't seem to mind the cold, he's so happy, like a cat with cream. No, I mean a homeless man with cream. Poirot, look at that dog, doesn't it remind you of someone? A celebrity. Quick, look, we're passing by.\" [407] \"Carter, get up,\" Poirot said sternly. \"Otherwise, we'll miss our stop. You're always looking around, daydreaming.\" [408] I stood up as soon as we got off the car. \"You brought me out aimlessly wandering in London, so you can't blame me for enjoying the scenery,\" I told him. [409] Poirot suddenly stopped. \"Tell me, why won't you look at the three bodies in the hotel? What makes you unable to bear it?\" [410] \"Nothing. I've looked at them as many times as you have. In fact, I already checked them thoroughly before you came.\" [411] \"If you don't want to talk about it, just say so, my friend.\" [412] \"There's really nothing to talk about. I think that after doing what's necessary, no one would want to stand there and keep looking at the bodies. That's it.\" [413] \"No,\" Poirot said calmly, \"it's not like that.\" [414] I should have told him the reason, but for some reason, I couldn't. When I was five years old, my grandfather died. He was sick for a long time and lay in his room. My parents thought I needed to go to his room every day to visit him because it was important to my grandfather. I didn't want to go, but I went every day to make my parents happy and to make my grandfather happy. I watched his skin turn yellow, listened to his breathing weaken, and watched his eyes lose their shine. I didn't pay attention to these things at the time, and I wasn't afraid. I just remember that every time I was in that room, I counted the seconds until I could stop, leave, and close the door. [415] On the day my grandfather died, I felt like I had been released from prison and was free again. He would soon be taken away, and there would be no more death in the house. But my mother said I had to go to my grandfather's room one last time, and she would come with me, and it would be fine. [416] The doctor laid his body flat, and my mother explained why he did so. I counted the time silently, longer than usual, at least one hundred and thirty seconds. I stood next to my mother, quietly looking at my grandfather's motionless, frail body. Suddenly, my mother said to me, \"Edward, hold his hand.\" I said I didn't want to, and she started crying, tears that seemed to never stop. [417] I had to hold my grandfather's bony hand. I would do anything to let go and escape that room. But I held on, until my mother stopped crying and said we could go downstairs, and then I let go. [418] Hold his hand, Edward, hold his hand. [419] Chapter 5\n[420] The Hundred Person Survey\n[421] When Polo and I walked into the Brawnshan Hotel's restaurant, we hardly noticed the crowd inside.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThis Richard, who later realized that he should not condemn and persecute a person for a human weakness, would he do that? This Richard, who rigidly believed that all criminals should be severely punished, thus breaking off his engagement with Ada Gransbury, would he let Nancy Drew, an innocent woman who only mistakenly fell in love with a man who didn't belong to her, bear the burden of three lives and face the legal consequences? Pfft, nonsense! It doesn't make sense. This is Jenny Hobbs' delusion, to mislead us again.\" [1987] I listened to this speech with my mouth half open. \"Poirot, are you sure? I believe her.\" [1988] \"Of course I'm sure. Henry Nixes didn't tell us that his brother Richard Nixes has been living like a recluse at home for sixteen years, never seeing anyone and never speaking to anyone? But Jenny Hobbs says that he has been persuading Harriet Spear and Ada Gransbury for sixteen years to take responsibility for the deaths of Patrick and Francis Eves and to pay the price. If Richard Nixes had been regularly in contact with the two women from Gleghorn, how could his brother Henry Nixes not notice that?\" [1989] \"That's a good point, but I don't agree.\" [1990] \"That's not the point. You must have noticed that Jenny's story is fundamentally wrong.\" [1991] \"It is certainly wrong to frame an innocent person for murder.\" [1992] \"Carter, I'm not talking about moral 'wrongness', but about factual impossibility. Are you trying to provoke me into explaining it clearly? Okay, let me remind you of a detail that may help you understand everything. According to Janie Hobbs, how did the keys to rooms 121 and 317 of the Blaukshan Hotel end up in Nancy Drew's blue coat pocket?\" [1993] \"Samuel Kid put them there to frame Nancy.\" [1994] \"Did he slip them in on the street?\" [1995] \"I think it's easy.\" [1996] \"Yes, but how did Samuel Kid get those two keys? It should have been Jenny who found the keys, went to room 238 to kill Richard Nigg, and then left with three keys, locked the door, and then gave the keys to Samuel Kid. But according to Jenny, she didn't go to Richard Nigg's room that night, nor did she go to the Brooks Han Hotel. She said that Mr. Nigg locked the door from the inside, hid the keys behind a loose tile in the fireplace, and then committed suicide. So, how did Samuel Kid get those two keys?\" [1997] I waited for Poirot to continue, but he didn't. So I said, \"I don't know.\" [1998] \"Maybe because Jenny Hobbs didn't come, Samuel Kid and Richard Nigg came up with an idea: the former killed the latter and took the keys to rooms 121 and 317 from Richard Nigg's room? But why didn't he take the key to his own room? Why did he still hide it behind a loose tile in the fireplace? The only reasonable explanation is that Richard Nigg committed suicide, but he wanted his suicide to look like a murder. My friend, but it's easy to do, just let Samuel Kid take the room key. That way, you don't have to open the window and don't have to fake the escape of the killer through the window.\" [1999] I found Poirot's argument convincing. \"Since Richard Nigg locked the door from the inside, how did Samuel Kid get into room 238, take the keys to rooms 121 and 317?\" [2000] \"Yes.\" [2001] \"What if he climbed a tree first and then climbed in through the window?\" [2002] \"Ketchup, think. Jenny Hobbs said she didn't go to the Blaxland Hotel that night, so either Samuel Kid and Richard Nigges collaborated to complete the plan, or the two men didn't cooperate. If they didn't cooperate, Mr. Kid didn't have to climb into Mr. Nigges' room through the window and take those two keys. It doesn't make sense. If these two men really collaborated, Samuel Kid should have put the three keys in Nancy Duane's pocket, not two. And one more thing... if Richard Nigges committed suicide, as you believe, so the cufflinks slid to his throat. But who arranged his body so straight? Do you really believe that a person who took poison could still meticulously design their own death pose? No! It's absolutely impossible.\" [2003] \"I need to think about this again.\" I said. \"You've raised so many new questions that I didn't have before.\" [2004] \"Such as?\" [2005] \"Why did the three victims order sandwiches, cakes, and scones but not eat them? Since they didn't eat, why was there nothing on Ada Gransberry's plate? Where did the food go?\" [2006] \"Ah! Now you're thinking like a real detective. Hercule Poirot is teaching you how to use your gray brain cells.\" [2007] \"Did you think of the food-related questions?\" [2008] \"Of course. I didn't mention the food issue when I asked Jenny to explain the contradictions because I wanted her to think we believed her story. So, I couldn't ask her any questions she couldn't answer.\" [2009] \"Polo! Samuel Kid's face!\" [2010] \"Where is it, my friend?\" [2011] \"No, I don't mean I saw his face. I mean... do you remember when we first met him at the 'Happy Coffee Shop'? He shaved his face, leaving a small patch of stubble with a small wound, while the rest was covered by thick stubble.\" [2012] Poirot nodded. [2013] \"What if the wound we saw wasn't from shaving, but from being scratched by a branch? What if Samuel Kidd scraped his face while climbing into or out of the window of Room 238? He knew he was telling us a lie, that he saw Nancy Duane fleeing the hotel, but he didn't want us to associate the mysterious wound on his face with the tree outside Richard Nigus's room window, so he shaved off a small patch of stubble around the wound.\" [2014] \"So we would think he hurt himself while shaving and only shaved a part of it. Poirot said, \"Then, when he came to see me at his residence, his entire beard was gone, but there were wounds on his face, meant to remind me that he could hurt himself while shaving. Yes, that way, I would think every wound on his face was from shaving.\" [2015] \"Why aren't you excited?\" I asked. [2016] \"Because it's obvious. I came to this conclusion two hours ago.\" [2017] \"Oh.\" I was disappointed. \"Wait, if Samuel Kid's wound was from the tree outside Richard Nigel's room window, then it means he could have climbed into the room, taken the keys to Rooms 121 and 317, right?\" [2018] \"There's no time to discuss what this means now,\" Poirot said sternly. \"We've arrived at the train station. But your question shows that you weren't listening to what I was saying.\" [2019] Dr. Ambrose Flavard was tall and muscular, in his fifties, with black hair that was starting to turn gray at the temples. His shirt was wrinkled and missing a button. Without further ado, he led us into the vicarage. We stood in a cold, large hall with high ceilings and cracked wooden floors. The entire house seemed to have been turned into a makeshift hospital to care for the sole patient. The door opened, and a nurse in uniform emerged. In a different situation, I might have been curious about this arrangement, but now all I could think about was Margaret Ernst. [2020] The entire house seemed to have been turned into a makeshift hospital, for the sole purpose of caring for the only patient. The door opened, and a nurse in uniform emerged. In a different situation, I might have been curious about this arrangement, but now all I could think about was Margaret Ernst. [2021] As soon as we were introduced, I asked, \"How is she?\" [2022] The doctor's face contorted with pain. He composed himself and said, \"She only let me say that she's fine.\" [2023] \"Who?\" Poirot asked. [2024] \"Margaret. She can't tolerate failure.\" [2025] \"You said she wanted to see us, is that true?\" [2026]Dr. Flavard paused for a moment, nodding slightly, saying, \"Most people wouldn't last this long after such a severe attack. Margaret is strong and determined, but the situation is still serious. But damn it, I'll do everything I can to heal her.\" [2027]\"What happened to her?\" [2028]\"Two scoundrels, who ran from the other end of the village to the churchyard at night... oh, I'm ashamed to repeat what they did to the gravestones of the Ives. Margaret heard the commotion outside, she's always alert even when she sleeps, she heard the sound of metal and stones colliding. She rushed out to try to stop them, and they attacked her with the shovel they brought along. They didn't care if they killed her or not! Hours later, the village police arrested them, and everything was cleared up.\" [2029]Polo said, \"Wait a minute, doctor. Do you know who attacked Mrs. Enst? Those two scoundrels... did they confess?\" [2030]\"Of course,\" Dr. Flavard gritted his teeth. [2031] \"So they were arrested?\" [2032] \"Yes, the police have arrested them.\" [2033] \"Who are they?\" I asked. [2034] \"Fred Krieger and Tobias Klatt, father and son. A pair of alcoholics, loafers.\" [2035] I wondered if the son was the loafer I had seen drinking with Walter Stuker at the King's Head Inn. (I later found out that it was indeed him.) [2036] \"They say Margaret obstructed them. As for the graves of the Ives...\u201d Dr. Flavard turned to me, \"Please understand, I'm not trying to blame you, but your visit was the trigger. Someone saw you go to Margaret's cottage, and all the villagers knew she was on the side of the Ives. They knew that Patrick Ives, whom you heard in Margaret's house, was not a lecherous fraud but a victim of cruel persecution and defamation\u2014and the perpetrators were them. This made them want to punish Patrick again. Patrick is dead, and they can't reach him anymore, so they defile his grave. Margaret always said that something like this would happen, so she sat by the window all day, hoping to catch and stop them. Do you know, she never saw Patrick and Francis Ives? Did she tell you? They were my friends. Their tragic fate made me sad, and I always felt guilty about their unjust treatment. And from the beginning, Margaret cared about this matter very much. She was shocked that such a thing happened in the parish her husband managed. She also made her husband care about it. Margaret and Charles coming to Gleeholm is truly fortunate. She is the best ally I could have. No, they are.\" [2037] \"Can I talk to Margaret?\" I asked. If she was dying\u2014although the doctor swore she wouldn't\u2014I had a feeling she had something to say before she passed away. [2038] \"Of course,\" Ambrose Flavard said. \"She would be angry if I didn't let you see her.\" [2039] I, Poirot, and the nurse followed the doctor up a wooden staircase without carpet, entering a bedroom. Margaret Enst's face was wrapped in bandages, covered in blood, bruises, and purple spots. I tried not to show my surprise, but tears welled up in my eyes. [2040] \"Ambrose, have they come?\" she asked. [2041] \"They have.\" [2042] \"Good day, Mrs. Enst. I am Hercule Poirot. I am deeply sorry for what has happened to you.\" [2043] \"Please call me Margaret, Mr. Carruthers. Is Mr. Cattermole here too?\" [2044] \"Yes, I am here.\" 