'liars,' but Charles did. His health was deteriorating, and if I hadn't been so stubborn in fighting the villains in the village, Charles might still be alive.\" [2072] \"Where are the suicide notes written by the Ivorys?\" Poirot asked. [2073] \"I don't know. I gave them to Margaret sixteen years ago, and I haven't asked her about them since.\" [2074] \"I burned them.\" [2075] \"Margaret.\" Ambrose Vlavard rushed to her bedside. \"You're awake.\" [2076] \"I remember every word on the note. It felt important to remember, so I did.\" [2077] \"You need to rest, Maggie. Talking will tire you out.\" [2078] \"Patrick's suicide note says, 'Tell Nancy I'll always love her.' But I didn't tell Nancy. If I had, it would have exposed Ambrose's lie during the autopsy. However... now the truth is out, Ambrose, you must tell Nancy what Patrick said.\" [2079] \"I will, don't worry, Maggie. I'll take care of everything.\" [2080] \"I'm worried. You haven't told Kitcher and Mr. Poirot about the threats Harry made after Patrick and Francis were buried. Tell them now.\" She closed her eyes again and fell asleep a few seconds later. [2081] \"What threat, doctor?\" Poirot asked. [2082] \"One day, Harriet Sipper came to the vicarage with a dozen or so hooligans, claiming that the villagers of Gleeholm were going to dig up Patrick and Frances' bodies. She said, 'A suicide does not have the right to be buried in such a holy place, it's God's rule.' Margaret walked to the door and said she was talking nonsense, and told them, 'That was the rule once, but it's not anymore. It was abolished in 1880, and it's now 1913. After death, a person's soul is entrusted to the merciful God, and no longer subject to human judgment.'\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nI often think that the light from the coffee shop can illuminate the entire alley, and if someone sees the light, they might come in to sit. Especially if they see you through the window.\" [1478] \"Especially if they see you through the window.\" I flattered her. [1479] She widened her eyes and said, \"That's it.\" [1480] \"What does that mean?\" [1481] \"After I asked Jenny to close the door, she rushed to the window and stared outside, as if someone was chasing her. She just stared at the window, staring, but she could only see herself, this room, and me - I mean my reflection. And I saw her and recognized her. You can ask Poirot, he knows this part too. Before she turned around, I said, 'Oh, it's you.' You see, if the caf\u00e9 is lit up and it's pitch black outside, the glass of the window will act like a mirror. You might think she was trying to see what was outside, but it wasn't like that.\u201d\n[1482] \"What does that mean?\" [1483] \"She wasn't looking outside to see if someone was chasing her, she was looking at me, just like I was looking at her. I could see her reflection, and she could see mine, like looking in a mirror. Can you understand that?\" [1484] I nodded and said, \"As long as you can see someone in the mirror, they can see you too.\" [1485] \"Absolutely correct. And Jenny was looking at me then, I swear, she was waiting to see what I would say or do, like observing. Mr. Cattermole, this may sound ridiculous, but at that time, I felt like I could see more than her. I could tell what she was thinking, and it sounded like a crazy idea. But I swear, she was waiting for me to serve her.\u201d\n[1486] \"Any rational person who comes here would wait for you to serve them.\" I smiled. [1487] \"Pfft.\" Phyllida made a disapproving noise, \"I don't know why I forgot about this. I swear, I didn't make it up... The girl in the glass was staring at my eyes, as if...,\" Phyllida furrowed her brow, \"as if I was the dangerous one, not someone on the street outside. But why was she looking at me like that? Can you understand that? I can't.\" [1488] I went to Scotland Yard first, and when I got back to the apartment, I met Poirot at the door, preparing to leave. He stood by the door, holding his coat and hat, with a flushed face and an anxious look, as if he couldn't keep his composure. Normally, nothing could bother him. Mrs. Bunchworth was also acting strange, ignoring me and complaining that the car hadn't arrived yet. Her face was also flushed. [1489] \"We must go to the Bluxton Hotel now, Mr. Cattermole. As soon as the car arrives, we'll leave,\" Poirot said, running his gloved fingers through his mustache. [1490] \"The car should have been here ten minutes ago,\" Mrs. Bunchworth said. \"Although it's late, you can take Mr. Cattermole with you.\" [1491] \"Is there an emergency?\" I asked him. [1492] \"Another murder,\" Poirot said. \"At the Brawley's Hotel.\" [1493] \"Oh, dear,\" I felt a momentary rush of shame and panic coursing through my veins. It had happened: a neat row of bodies. One, two, three, four...\n[1494] Eight lifeless hands, palm down...\n[1495] Hold his hand, Edward...\n[1496] \"Is it Jean Hobby?\" I asked Poirot, as if I could hear blood rushing in my ears. [1497] I should have taken his warnings more seriously. How could I have been so careless? [1498] \"I don't know. Ah! So you know her name too. Mr. Lazarus called to inform us. The car is finally here.\" [1499] As I turned to walk away, I felt someone tugging at my sleeve. It was Mrs. Brandonsworthy who was holding onto my sleeve. She said, \"Be careful at the hotel, Mr. Carruthers. If you two get hurt again, I'll be completely undone.\" [1500] \"Of course, I will.\" [1501] She wore an expression of great pain and said, \"If I were you, I wouldn't go there. That man, the one who was just killed, what was he doing there? Three people have been killed at the Brawnsham Hotel in the past week! If he didn't want to die, why didn't he go somewhere else? It's reckless, ignoring the danger signs and causing trouble for you and your friends.\" [1502] I should repeat these words verbatim to the corpse, I thought to myself. It might make me feel better if I could say them with a smile. [1503] Mrs. Blanche continued to advise, \"Tell the other guests there are two vacant rooms here, not as luxurious as the Brawnsham Hotel, but they'll wake up alive tomorrow.\" [1504] \"Carrington, hurry up!\" Poirot called from the car. [1505] I handed my luggage to Mrs. Blanche and quickly got into the car. [1506] As soon as the car started, Poirot said, \"My friend, I wish I could avoid the fourth murder, but I failed.\" [1507] \"I don't think so,\" I said. [1508] \"What?\" [1509] \"You did your best. The killer succeeded, but that doesn't mean you failed.\" [1510] He looked down on me and said, \"If you think that, you're the favorite of the murderers. It's clear I failed!\" He raised his hand to stop me from speaking, \"Please don't say such stupid things. Tell me about your situation in Grellingsholme. Besides Jenny's surname, did you find anything else?\" [1511]I told him the whole trip, and as I spoke, I felt myself slowly coming back to normal. I didn't miss any details, as every little detail might be related to the case for someone as meticulous as Poirot. While speaking, I noticed something strange: Poirot's eyes were getting greener and greener. It was as if someone turned on a flashlight inside his head, making his eyes glow. [1512]After listening to me, he said, \"So, Patrick Yves was a dormitory supervisor for Jenny when he was studying at Jesus College, Oxford. Interesting.\" [1513]\"Why?\" [1514]He didn't answer my question but asked another one. [1515]\"Did you secretly follow Margaret Ernst after your first visit to her house?\" [1516] \"Follow her? No. I felt she would not go anywhere. She seemed to be staring out the window at the grave of the Yve couple all the time.\" [1517] \"You should have thought of where she might go or who might go to her. Think, Cattermole. The day she refused to tell you anything about Patrick and Francis Yve, she said, 'Come back tomorrow.' The next day you went, and she told you the whole story. Didn't you ever wonder why she delayed telling you? Maybe she wanted to consult someone?\" [1518] \"No, I never thought of that. She seemed like a woman who would carefully consider things before making a major decision and not one who would seek advice from friends. So, I didn't suspect anything.\" [1519] \"But I do suspect,\" said Poirot. \"I suspect Margaret Ernst wanted to consult with Dr. Ambrose Flavard about what she should say.\" [1520] \"Well, if she did consult someone, it would only be him,\" I admitted. \"She did mention his name several times during our conversation. It was clear she admired him.\" [1521] \"But you didn't investigate Dr. Ambrose,\" Poirot said, a little angry. \"You were too loyal to your oath. And also, it's the British politeness that makes you say 'worship' instead of 'love.' Your description clearly shows that Margaret Ernst loves Dr. Ambrose Flavard! She never met the Evans couple, yet she passionately talked about them? No, it was her passion for Dr. Flavard. The Evans couple and the doctor were good friends, and their tragic death made him very sad, and she was sad because of his sadness. Do you understand, Cader?\" [1522] I grumbled in response, not caring. In my opinion, Margaret Ernst was a woman who only cared about rules and was crazy about the injustice that happened to the Evans couple, but I knew it was stupid to say it out loud. Poirot would lecture me about not understanding the art of love. To avoid him continuing to dwell on my mistakes and shortcomings, I told him that I went to the \"Joy Cafe\" and repeated what Phoebe said. \"What do you think that means?\" I asked, and the car suddenly bumped, as if it had run over something on the road. [1523] Poirot ignored my question and asked if I had finished telling everything. [1524] \"Yes, I've told you everything about Mr. Glengower. Another new piece of information I got today is that the autopsy results are out, and just as we expected, all three victims died of cyanide poisoning. But there's a mystery, there's no food residue in their stomachs. Harryette Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nix, all three were killed several hours before they had any food. That means, the afternoon tea disappeared.\" [1525] \"Ah! This mystery is already solved.\" [1526] \"Solved? Should I say that this puzzle has been created, right?\" [1]\n[1527] \"Oh, Ketteridge, if I tell you the answer, because I pity you, I won't let you exercise your independent thinking ability - but you must think! I have a good friend, and I've never told you about him before. His name is Hastings. I often ask him to use his gray cells, although I know he can never match me.\" [1528] I thought he was going to flatter me, but he continued, \"On the other hand, you... you're the same, you're always inferior to me. You're not intellectually deficient, not insensitive, and not lacking in imagination, but you lack confidence. You don't want to find the answer yourself, but instead, you look for someone who can tell you the answer - well, you found Hercule Poirot! But Poirot not only solves puzzles, my friend, but he is also a guide, a teacher. He hopes that you can learn to think for yourself, just like the woman you mentioned, Margaret Ernst. She believes in her own judgment, not the Bible.\" [1529] \"Yes, I think she's arrogant,\" I pointed out sharply. I wanted to say more, but the car had arrived at the Bluxton Hotel. [1530] * * *\n[1531][1] The original sentence of Poirot was \"That is one mystery solved.\" The translated sentence is \"It was a mystery created.\" The sentence structure corresponds to the previous sentence. [1532] Chapter 15\n[1533] The Fourth Pin\n[1534] As soon as we arrived at the Blaukhahn Hotel lobby, we met Henry Nigges, Richard Nigges' younger brother. He was holding a briefcase in one hand and a very large