 in the hotel? She could be anywhere!\" Lazari exclaimed mournfully. [1545] \"You don't know where she is?\" As we followed the hotel manager to the elevator, Poirot asked him, \"What does that mean? She's not here, is she? Not in this hotel?\" [1546] Raphael Pabak pushed a large trolley towards us, filled with sheets to be washed. He lowered his head to greet us and stopped. \"Mr. Poirot, I've been thinking, wondering if I could remember anything else from the night of the incident in Room 317.\" [1547] \"And then?\" Poirot asked eagerly. [1548] \"But I can't remember anything, sir. I'm sorry.\" [1549] \"It's okay, thank you, Mr. Pabak.\" [1550] \"Look,\" Lazari said, \"the elevator is here, but now I'm afraid to get in! It's in my own hotel! I don't know what I'll see next, or what I won't see. I'm afraid to turn corners, afraid to open doors... I'm afraid of the shadows in the hallway, afraid of the creaking floorboards...\"\n[1551] When we entered the elevator, Poirot tried to make sense of the hotel manager's agitated words but found nothing. Lazari seemed unable to form a complete sentence: \"Miss Jenny Hobbs booked a room...what? Yes, with blonde hair...But where did she go?...Yes, a brown hat...We lost her!...She didn't bring any luggage...I saw it with my own eyes...But I arrived too late!...What? Yes, a coat, a light brown...\u201d\n[1552] When we reached the fifth floor, Lazari led us briskly down the hallway. \"Harriet Sipper is on the second floor, remember?\" I said to Poirot. \"Richard Nixes is on the third floor, and Ada Gransbury is on the fourth. I think there's something fishy about that.\" [1553] By the time we caught up to Lazari, he had already opened the door to Room 402. \"Gentlemen, in the beautiful Browns Hotel, you are about to witness the ugliest scene. Please prepare yourselves.\" [1554] \"But...where is the body?\" I asked. Unlike the previous cases, there was no body lying there, and I felt relieved. [1555] \"No one knows where the body is, Kattarpillow,\" Poirot's voice was calm but tinged with anger or fear. [1556] There was a large pool of blood on the floor between a chair and a lounge table - exactly where the corpse would have been in rooms 121, 238, and 317 - long bloodstains extending to the other side of the room, as if something had been dragged along. Was it the body of Jane Hobbs? The shape of the bloodstains suggested an arm. There were some separated lines, like traces left by fingers...\n[1557] This sight was disgusting, and I couldn't help but turn my face away. [1558] \"Poirot, come quickly.\" In a corner of the room, there was a deep brown hat, upside down. Inside the hat was a small piece of metal. Was it...? [1559] \"It's Jane's hat,\" Poirot's voice trembled slightly. \"Carrington, my worst fears have come true. The hat contains...\u201d He slowly paced back and forth. \"Yes, as I suspected, it's a cufflink. The fourth cufflink, with the initials PJI.\" [1560] His mustache was quivering violently, and I knew that Poirot was in great pain. \"Poirot is a fool, a complete fool, to let such a thing happen!\" [1561] \"Poirot, no one will blame you\u2026\" I don't finish my sentence. [1562] \"No! Don't console me! You're just trying to avoid pain and setbacks, but I'm not like you, Carter! I don't approve of this\u2026 cowardly behavior. Don't try to stop me anymore, I should regret this. It's necessary!\" [1563] I stand there like a statue, unmoving. He succeeded in not letting me speak. [1564] \"Carter,\" he suddenly calls me, as if to regain my attention, \"come and look at the bloodstains. The body was dragged, leaving this\u2026 trail. What do you see?\" [1565] \"Er\u2026 yes, I think so.\" [1566] \"Look at the direction the body was moved: not towards the window, but in the opposite direction.\" [1567] \"What does that mean?\" I asked. [1568] \"Since Jenny's body isn't here, it must have been moved out of this room. These bloodstains indicate that the direction of movement isn't towards the window, but towards the corridor, so\u2026\"\n[1569] \"So?\" I asked tentatively. Suddenly, my thoughts cleared up. \"Oh, I understand your meaning now: those traces, those bloodstains, are what the killer left behind when dragging Jenny Hobbs' body out of the pool of blood towards the door, right?\" [1570] \"No. Look at the width of the door, Cader, look, the door is wide. What can that tell you?\" [1571] \"I don't see anything,\" I said, thinking it best to be honest about my thoughts.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2248] \"You also told me that Ada or Harriet said something else, but you can't remember. Then the person you thought was Richard Nigg said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does. I don't agree with what you just said about 'old enough to be his mother.' At that point, the woman called Harriet smiled and said, 'Well, we can't prove who's right, can we? Let's agree to disagree.' Is that right?\" [2249] Raphael Burbank confirmed Poirot's recollection was accurate. [2250] \"Good. Mr. Burbank, I believe that the line you can't remember was said by Harriet, and I'm one hundred percent sure that in that room, you didn't hear Ada Gransbury say a single word! And you didn't see her face because she was sitting with her back to the door.\" [2251] Baxter furrowed his brow, concentrated for a moment, and then said, \"I think you're right, Mr. Poirot. Yes, I didn't see Miss Edith Gransbury's face. And... after your reminder, I remember that I didn't hear her speak.\" [2252] \"Of course you didn't hear her speak, sir. The reason is simple: the Edith Gransbury you saw at 7:15, sitting with her back to the door and slumped in the chair, was already dead. There was a corpse in Room 317 when you brought the afternoon tea!\" [2253] Chapter 24\n[2254] The Blue Pot and Bowl\n[2255] There were a few screams of terror in the dining room, and I almost screamed myself. It was too strange! Thanks to my work at Scotland Yard, I've seen many corpses, but none were as terrifying as the sight of a dead woman sitting upright, as if she were enjoying tea with her friends. [2256] Poor Raphael Burbak trembled all over and his lips quivered uncontrollably. He had been so close to the monster that no sane person would have done the same. [2257] \"That's why the afternoon tea had to be delivered to Miss Ada Gransbury's room,\" Poirot continued. \"Room 238, where Mr. Richard Nigges lived, should have been the most convenient meeting place for the three victims, as it was between the other two rooms. And, without Mr. Nigges having to say anything, the afternoon tea would naturally be charged to his account. But they couldn't let Raphael Burbak see the three victims alive at seven fifteen in Room 238! If they had, they would have had to remove Miss Ada Gransbury's corpse from her room, carry it through the hotel corridor, and transport it to the lower-level Room 238. That would have been too risky and someone would have seen it.\" [2258] The faces of the bewildered people were now filled with shock. I imagine that Luca Lazzari might soon need to hire new staff. I certainly don't want to come back to the Brawnsham Hotel after the case is over. I imagine that most of the people in this room must feel the same way. [2259] Poirot continued to explain: \"Ladies and gentlemen, please carefully recall Mr. Richard Nigges' generosity. Oh, he was so generous, insisting on paying for the tea and paying for a car to pick up Harriet and Ada from the station. Why didn't they come together on the train and then share a car to the hotel? Since Mr. Richard Nigges knew that he, Harriet, and Ada were all about to die, why did he care so much about whether the food and drink bills were charged to his name?\" [2260] This was a good question. Each point Poirot made was on target and something I should have thought of. Regardless, I hadn't noticed how many inconsistencies there were in Miss Hobbs' statement. How could I have overlooked so many obvious gaps? [2261] Poirot said, \"The person pretending to be Richard Nigges was only trying to make Rafael Borker see him at 7:15 and 7:30 to see Thomas Brine. He didn't care about the bills! He knew that he and his accomplices wouldn't have to pay and that he had taken the food outside. So, how did he take the food out? He put it in a suitcase! Canterbury, do you remember seeing a homeless man near the hotel when we took the bus? The homeless man was eating from a suitcase, do you remember? You said he was 'a homeless man with cream.' Tell me, did you see him eating cream clearly?\" [2262] \"Oh, my goodness! Yes, I saw! He was eating a piece... of cake with cream inside.\" [2263] Poirot nodded and said, \"He found a discarded suitcase near Brookes Han hotel that contained tea for three people! Now, my friend, let me test your memory again. Do you remember, when I first came to Brookes Han, you told me that Miss Edith Gransberry brought a lot of clothes, enough to fill the closet? But her room only had one suitcase, the same size as Richard Nigges's and Harriet Sipper's, but they brought fewer clothes. When I asked you to put Miss Gransberry's clothes in her suitcase this afternoon, what did you find?\" [2264] \"It didn't fit. \" I felt like a big fool again. I felt foolish earlier because I couldn't even organize Miss Edith Gransberry's luggage, and now there's another reason. [2265] \"You were blaming yourself,\" Poirot said. \"You always like to criticize yourself, but in reality, it's impossible to fit all the clothes in, because they were brought in two suitcases. Even Hercule Poirot couldn't fit them into one suitcase.\" [2266] He turned around and said to the people in the restaurant, \"It was while throwing away the suitcase full of food and returning to the hotel that the man met Thomas Brinell, the receptionist of Blauxham, who is the door of this restaurant. Why did he deliberately discuss the bill with Brinell? There is only one reason: to deepen Brinell's impression and make him remember that Richard Nigges was still alive at half past seven. But the man who impersonated Mr. Nigges made a mistake. He said, 'Nigges can afford it, while Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransbury can't.' But that's not true! Henry Nigges, Richard's brother, can prove that Richard has no income and has little left in savings. But the man playing Richard Nigges didn't know that. He thought Richard was a gentleman, a former lawyer, and must have a lot of money.\" [2267] \"Henry Nigges first told me and Ketaki that since moving to Devonshire, his brother Richard has become gloomy and preoccupied, like a recluse with no desire for life. Mr. Henry Nigges, am I right?\" [2268] \"Yes, that's right.\" [2269] \"A recluse! Would a recluse like sherry and cake, would he chat with two women in a luxurious hotel in London? No! The man who took the afternoon tea from Rafael Bocaccio and the sherry from Thomas Brinell was not Richard Nigges. That man complimented Mr. Brinell on his efficiency and said something like this: 'You're efficient, and I believe you can get the job done - charge the cost of food and drink to my account, Richard Nigges in room 238 at Blauxham.' He chose his words carefully to make Thomas Brinell believe this Richard Nigges, and they must have met before. Mr. Brinell may have felt guilty at the time because he couldn't remember meeting this Mr. Nigges before, so he decided to remember him this time. He wanted to remember that he had met this man at least twice. Of course, working in a big hotel in London, he has to meet hundreds of people every day! I'm sure there are often cases where he remembers their names and appearances, but they forget what they look like - for him, they're just 'guests'!\" [2270] \"Excuse me, Mr. Poirot, forgive me,\" Luca Lazzari rushed up and said, \"generally speaking, you are right, but there are exceptions. Thomas Brinell is an exception, he has a super memory and can remember guests' faces and names very well. He has a super memory!\" [2271] Poirot smiled approvingly and said, \"Is that so? Well, it seems I'm right.\" [2272] \"About what?\" I asked. [2273] \"Please be patient, Ketton-Cremer, I'll explain one by one. The day before the murder, on Wednesday, when Richard Nix checked into the hotel, the man impersonating him was in the lobby. He might have been casing the place for his upcoming impersonation. Anyway, he saw Richard Nix. How did he recognize Richard Nix? I'll get to that in a moment. He saw Thomas Breen check him in and hand him the room key. The next evening, after the man impersonating Nix took the afternoon tea and disposed of the food outside, on his way back to Room 317, he met Thomas Breen. He was a quick-witted man who immediately saw an excellent opportunity to further mislead the police. He approached Breen, greeted him, and claimed to be Richard Nix, reminding Breen that they had met before. [2274] \"In fact, Thomas Breen had never met this man before, but he remembered the name Richard Nix because he had given him the room key. When suddenly someone spoke to him in a confident, friendly, and intimate manner, claiming to be Richard Nix, Thomas Breen naturally believed he was Richard Nix. Although he couldn't remember the face, he would only blame himself for being careless.\" [2275] Thomas Breen's face turned as red as wine. [2276] Poirot continued, \"The imposter who pretended to be Richard Nigges ordered another sherry. Why? To prolong his meeting with Thomas Brineau and make him remember him? Or to calm his nerves with alcohol? Both are possible.\" [2277] \"Now, allow me to change the subject. We found cyanide in the remaining sherry, just like in the cups of Miss Harriet Sipper and Miss Ada Gransbury. However, it was not the tea or sherry that killed these three victims. It couldn't have been. The murders had already occurred when the drinks were delivered to the rooms. The poisoned sherry glass and two tea cups were placed on the small table next to the body to create a false crime scene, leading us to believe the incident occurred after 7:15 p.m. In fact, Miss Harriet Sipper, Miss Ada Gransbury, and Mr. Richard Nigges were poisoned earlier using a different method. There is a glass cup by the sink in each room, isn't there, Mr. Lazari?\" [2278] \"Yes, Mr. Poirot, there is one in each room.\" [2279] \"So, I think they must have ingested the poison \u2013 in water. Afterward, the killer cleaned the glass that held the poison and placed it back by the sink. Mr. Brineau! Poirot suddenly called out, causing the reception assistant to shrink his neck, as if someone had shot him, \"You don't like talking in public, but when we first gathered in this restaurant, you mustered the courage to tell us that you had encountered Mr. Nigges in the corridor. But you didn't mention the sherry, even though I specifically asked about it. Later, you came to me and added the sherry incident. I asked you why you didn't mention it before, and you didn't answer. I didn't understand the reason at the time, but my friend, Caderousse, said some very insightful words that inspired me. He said you are a responsible person, and now involved in a murder case, you are concealing something that makes you very embarrassed and you are sure it has nothing to do with the case. He was right on the mark, wasn't he?\" [2280] Mr. Brineau nodded slightly. [2281] \"Allow me to explain,\" Poirot raised his voice, even though it was already quite loud, \"When we gathered in this restaurant last time, I asked everyone if they had delivered sherry to Mr. Nigus' room. No one spoke. Why didn't Thomas Brine say, 'I didn't deliver it to his room, but I did bring him a sherry'? Poirot will tell you! He didn't because he had doubts and didn't want to risk saying something uncertain. [2282] \"Among all the hotel staff, only Mr. Brine has seen one of the three victims \u2013 or rather, he was misled to believe he had seen Richard Nigus more than once. He knows he gave a man who claimed to be Richard Nigus a sherry, and the man acted as if he had seen him before, but he didn't look like the Richard Nigus he remembered. Don't forget, Mr. Lazari told us that Mr. Brine has excellent memory for names and faces. That's why he didn't speak when I asked about the sherry! He was thinking it through. A voice whispered in his head: 'It must be him, the same person. But it's not him, I can recognize him.'\" [2283] \"Thank God,\" said Thomas Brine. \"Poirot, every word you say is true.\" [2284] \"Let me explain further,\" Poirot raised his voice again, \"When we gathered in this restaurant last time, I asked everyone if they had delivered sherry to Mr. Nigus' room. No one spoke. Why didn't Thomas Brine say, 'I didn't deliver it to his room, but I did bring him a sherry'? Poirot will tell you! He didn't because he had doubts and didn't want to risk saying something uncertain. [2285] \"Among all the hotel staff, only Mr. Brine has seen one of the three victims \u2013 or rather, he was misled to believe he had seen Richard Nigus more than once. He knows he gave a man who claimed to be Richard Nigus a sherry, and the man acted as if he had seen him before, but he didn't look like the Richard Nigus he remembered. Don't forget, Mr. Lazari told us that Mr. Brine has excellent memory for names and faces. That's why he didn't speak when I asked about the sherry\n[2286] \"Of course,\" Poirot said without modesty. \"Please remember, ladies and gentlemen, the same name does not necessarily mean the same person. When Mr. Lazarus described the registration of Miss Jenny Hobbs to me, I also thought she was the same Miss Jenny Hobbs I had met at the 'Caf\u00e9 Joyeux.' It sounded similar, light hair, dark brown hat, light brown coat. But two men had only seen a woman fitting that description once, and they could not be sure they saw the same person. [2287] \"This point troubled me. I was already suspicious that the corpse of Richard Nigel I had seen at the crime scene and the living Richard Nigel that Mr. Raphael Barker and Mr. Thomas Brineau had seen on the night of the crime were two different men. Then I remembered that Mr. Thomas Brineau had checked in Mr. Richard Nigel on the Wednesday when he arrived at the Brawnsham Hotel. If I'm right, then this Richard Nigel is the real Richard Nigel. Suddenly, I understood Mr. Thomas Brineau's dilemma. How could he say in front of everyone that a man seemed to have two faces? They would think he was crazy! [2288] \"You're half crazy now, Mr. Poirot,\" Samuel Kidder sneered. [2289] Poirot ignored him and continued, \"The imposter may not look very similar to Richard, but I am certain he can imitate his voice very well. He is very good at imitation - Mr. Kidder, isn't he? [2290] \"Don't believe this man! He's a fraud!\" [2291] \"No, Mr. Kidder, you're the fraud. You've impersonated me more than once.\" [2292] At this point, Phyllis Springle, sitting in the back of the restaurant, stood up and said, \"You should all believe Mr. Poirot. He's telling the truth, and it's all fact. I've heard Samuel Kidder mimic his accent before. With my eyes closed, I couldn't tell the difference.\" [2293] \"Samuel Kidder doesn't just deceive people with his voice,\" Poirot said. \"When I first met him, he pretended to be a sloppy, intellectually challenged man. His shirt was stained, and he had lost a button. His beard was half-shaved. Mr. Kidder, tell these people why you went to such lengths to make yourself look so unkempt?\" [2294] Samuel Kidder stared resolutely ahead, saying nothing, his eyes filled with disgust. [2295] \"Well, if you don't want to say, I'll explain for you. Mr. Kidder escaped from the window of Richard Nigus's room 238 and climbed down. His face was scratched by the branches outside. A well-dressed, well-groomed man with a scratch on his face would be conspicuous and arouse suspicion, wouldn't he? A well-dressed person wouldn't leave a scar from a razor. Mr. Kidder didn't want me to think along these lines, didn't want me to guess that he had recently climbed out of a window and down a tree, so he created an unkempt appearance. He pretended he couldn't shave himself properly, that he would hurt himself if he tried, to avoid causing more scratches. Poirot would think it was only natural for such a sloppy man to cut himself with a razor. That's what I initially thought. \" [2296] \"Wait, Poirot,\" I said. \"If you say that Samuel Kid climbed out of Richard Nigges' window...\"\n[2297] \"Does it mean he killed Mr. Nigges? No, he didn't. He helped the murderer kill Richard Nigges. As for the murderer... I haven't gotten to that yet.\" [2298] \"Yes, you haven't gotten there yet,\" I said bluntly. \"You also didn't say who the three people were in Room 317 when Rafael Bacard sent the afternoon tea. You only said that the three victims were already dead at that time.\" [2299] \"Yes, at 7:15, the three people in Room 317 included the deceased Edith Gransbury. But she was placed on a chair, and if you didn't see her face, you would think she was still alive. The other one was Samuel Kid, who impersonated Richard Nigges.\" [2300] \"Yes, I guessed that, but who was the third person? The one who impersonated Harriet Sipper and talked about gossip? It couldn't have been Jane Hobbs. As you said, Jane was on her way to 'The Happy Coffee Shop' at that time.\" [2301] \"Ah, yes, the woman who loved to gossip,\" Poirot said. \"I'll tell you who she is, my friend. It was Nancy Duane.\" [2302] There was a chorus of shocked exclamations in the restaurant. [2303] \"Oh, no, Poirot sir,\" Luca Lazzari said.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"If the killer wanted to move the victim's body out of the hotel room, he wouldn't care about the width of the door.\" [1572] Poirot shook his head sadly and muttered under his breath. [1573] He turned to ask Razuri. \"Sir, tell me everything you know, from the beginning.\" [1574] \"Of course, certainly,\" Razuri cleared his throat. \"The guest staying in this room was named Jenny Hobbs. Mr. Poirot, she looked like she was in a hurry, rushed into the hotel, threw money on the front desk, and demanded a room, as if she was running away from a demon chasing her! I personally brought her to the room and wondered what to do next? Should I call the police and say that a woman named Jenny had checked into the hotel? Mr. Poirot, you specifically asked me about this name, but there are countless Jennys in London, not just one sad and troubled woman, and her reason for being sad may not be related to murder. How could I know she\u2014\"\n[1575] \"Sir, please get to the point,\" Poirot interrupted him. \"Then what did you do?\" [1576] \"I waited for about thirty minutes and then went up to the fifth floor, knocked on the door. But no one answered! So, I went back down to get the key.\" [1577] While Lazari was talking, I walked over to the window and looked outside. Any view was more pleasant than the bloodstain, the hat, and the dreadful monogrammed cufflink. The room faced the hotel garden, just like Richard Nigges' room on the second and third floors. I stared at the vast lawn but soon looked away because it felt like they were staring back at me: an endless expanse of still objects intertwined, like hand in hand. [1578] I was about to turn around to look at Poirot and Lazari when I noticed two people in the garden below the window, standing next to a brown handcart. I could only see the tops of their heads, a man and a woman, tightly embracing. The woman's head tilted to the side, as if she had almost stumbled and fallen. Her companion held her even closer. I stepped back quickly, but not quickly enough, and the man looked up at me. It was Thomas Brine, the young reception assistant. His face turned red. I took another step back, so the garden was completely out of sight. I thought to myself, poor Brine, he would be embarrassed enough to speak in front of everyone, imagine how embarrassed he would be to be seen with someone else. [1579] Lazari continued, \"I came back with the master key and knocked on the door again, to avoid barging into the privacy of this young lady. But no one answered! So, I opened the door myself... and then saw all this!\" [1580] \"Did Miss Jenny Hobbs specifically request a room on the fifth floor?\" I asked. [1581] \"No, she didn't. My reliable employee John Good was busy, so I personally led her to this room. Miss Hobbs said, 'Just give me any room, quickly! Please, hurry!'\" [1582] \"Has anyone left a note about the fourth murder at the front desk?\" Poirot asked. [1583] \"No, there was no note this time,\" Lazari said. [1584] \"Has anyone delivered food or drinks to this room, or has the guest requested anything?\" [1585] \"No, nothing.\" [1586] \"Have all the hotel staff checked this room?\" [1587] \"Yes, everyone has checked it. Poirot sir, we searched everywhere\u2026\"\n[1588] \"Sir, you said that Miss Jenny Hobbes is a young lady. How old do you think she is?\" [1589] \"Oh\u2026 I'm sorry. She's not particularly young, but she's not old either.\" [1590] \"About thirty years old?\" Poirot asked. [1591] \"I think she might be around forty, but it's hard to tell with women.\" [1592] Poirot nodded. \"Brown hat and a light brown coat, light yellow hair, looking anxious, around forty years old. The Jennie Hobbs you described sounds very similar to the Jennie Hobbs I met at the 'Joyful Coffee Shop' last Thursday evening, but how can we be sure it's the same person? Two different places, different witnesses\u2026\" Suddenly, he fell silent, but his lips kept moving. [1593] \"Poirot?\" I said. [1594] At that moment, his eyes turned green, and he stared at Lazari, saying, \"Sir, I must talk to that attentive waiter again, Mr. Rafael Puccini. And Thomas Brine and John Good. In fact, I must talk to every one of your employees again, asking each of them how many times they have seen Harriet Sippey, Richard Nigges, and Edith Gransbury, whether alive or dead.\" [1595] Clearly, he had found a key point. When I realized this, I was so tense that I could hear my own breathing, and I also had a major discovery. \"Poirot,\" I whispered. [1596] \"I just... \" I cleared my throat. For some reason, even opening my mouth was difficult. \"I just saw a woman in the hotel garden.\" [1597] I couldn't say that the woman was in Thomas Brignell's arms, nor could I describe her strange posture, looking as if she were about to fall, her head tilted to one side. It was too... peculiar. Doubt crept into my mind, but I felt embarrassed to say it out loud. [1598] \"Thank goodness, I can still mention an important detail.\" I told Poirot. \"She was wearing a light brown coat.\" [1599] Chapter Sixteen\n[1600] Responding to Lies with Lies\n[1601] \"Returning to the hotel after a few hours, Poirot found me engrossed in my crossword puzzle. 'Carter, why are you sitting in such a dark place? I think you can't even see the words you're supposed to write.'\" [1602] \"The firelight is bright enough, and I'm not writing at the moment. I'm thinking. I haven't made much progress, and I don't know how those who invented the crossword puzzle for the newspaper did it. I've been studying this game for months, but I still haven't finished it. I said, maybe you can help me. Can you think of a six-letter word that means death?\" [1603] \"Carter.\" Poirot's tone became even more stern. [1604] \"Hmm?\" I said. [1605] \"Do you think I'm a fool? Or are you a fool yourself? The word that represents death and has six letters is murder.\" [1606] \"Yes, that's the most obvious answer. That's what I thought at first.\" [1607] \"I'm glad to hear that, my friend.\" [1608] \"If 'murder' started with the letter D, it would be perfect. But it doesn't start with D, and I'm stuck on a word that starts with D...\"\n[1609] \"Forget about the crossword puzzle. We have much to discuss.\" [1610] \"I refuse to believe, and I won't believe, that Thomas Brine killed Jenny.\" I said firmly. [1611] \"You seem to sympathize with him,\" said Poirot. [1612] \"Yes, I would bet my entire fortune that he is not the murderer. Who says his girlfriend can't have a brown coat? Brown coats are quite common.\" [1613] \"He is a receptionist,\" said Poirot. \"Why would he stand next to a handcart in the garden?\" [1614] \"Perhaps the handcart was just there!\" [1615] \"And Mr. Brine and his girlfriend just happened to be standing next to it?\" [1616] \"Well, why not?\" I said, exasperated. \"Doesn't it sound more plausible that Burrnell planned to use the trolley to carry Janice Hobson's body out of the garden, only to find me looking out the window and pretending to embrace her? You could also say\u2026\" I stopped, took a deep breath, \"Oh, my God. Are you suggesting\u2026\"\n[1617] \"What, my friend? What do you think Poirot would say?\" [1618] \"Rafael Barker is a waiter, but why was he pushing a laundry cart?\" [1619] \"Right. And why was he pushing a laundry cart through the grand hall towards the front door? Shouldn't he have been washing it inside the hotel? If Mr. Lazari hadn't been so preoccupied with the missing fourth body, he would have noticed that. Of course, he wouldn't suspect Mr. Barker, all the employees were above suspicion in his eyes.\" [1620] \"Wait a minute,\" I said, putting the crossword puzzle aside. \"That's why you said the door was wide enough? The laundry cart could have easily fit into Room 402, but why didn't it? Why bother dragging the body?\" [1621] Poirot nodded approvingly. \"Yes, my friend, those are questions you should ask yourself.\" [1622] \"But\u2026do you really think it could have been Rafael Barker who killed Janie Hobbs, pushed her body out in a laundry cart, and just happened to pass by us? He even stopped to greet us!\" [1623] \"Indeed, he didn't say anything. What? Do you think it's unfair of me to suspect those who have helped us?\" [1624] \"Well\u2026\"\n[1625] \"Helping others is commendable, my friend, but it doesn't absolve one of the crime of murder. Since you're not satisfied with me, let me give you another scenario to consider: Mr. Henry Nigges, carrying a large suitcase. Large enough to fit a skinny woman's body inside.\" [1626]I covered my face with my hands. \"I can't take it anymore,\" I said. \"Henry Nix? No, I'm sorry, it wasn't him. He was in Devonshire that night. I believe he's completely trustworthy.\" [1627]\"You mean, he and his wife said they were in Devonshire that night,\" Poirot corrected me quickly. \"Let's go back to those bloodstains, which indicate that the body was dragged towards the door... Of course, you could have taken an empty suitcase to the center of the room and placed it next to the body. So, we must once again consider this question: why drag Jeanne Hobbs' body towards the door?\" [1628]\"Please, Poirot, if you must discuss this, let's do it at another time. Not now.\" [1629]My unease seemed to disappoint him, and he suddenly said, \"Well, since you're not in the mood to discuss these possibilities now, I'll tell you what happened in London while you were in Gleeholm. You might prefer facts.\" [1630]\"Yes, that would be better,\" I said. [1631] Polo smoothed his mustache and sat down in an armchair, starting to talk incessantly about the conversations he had with Rafael Borgia, Samuel Kid, Nancy Duane, and Louisa Wallace while I was in Grelling. By the time he finished, I was dizzy, so I took a risk and encouraged him to continue. \"Did you leave out any important details?\" [1632] \"Such as?\" [1633] \"Oh, the useless and clumsy maid of Louisa Wallace's house - Dolores. You emphasized that when you stood on the landing with her, you suddenly realized something important, but you didn't say what it was.\" [1634] \"Indeed, I didn't.\" [1635] \"And the mysterious painting, you said it was sent to Scotland Yard after it was finished. What was it about? What was Stanly Bill going to do with it?\" [1636] \"Ah, I didn't tell you that either.\" Poirot seemed a bit apologetic, as if he had no choice. [1637] I foolishly asked, \"And why do you want to know how many times each employee at the Bluxton Hotel has seen Richard Nix, Harriet Sipper, and Ada Gransby, whether alive or dead? What's the point of that? You didn't explain that either.\" [1638] \"Poirot, you left so many blanks!\" [1639] \"Don't forget the earlier questions you left unanswered. For instance, the murder at the Bluxton Hotel and the strange outburst by Jenny Hobbs at the 'Joyful Cafe' both had two extremely unusual characteristics. You also mentioned that they had two extremely unusual common points.\" [1640] \"I did say that, my friend, but I wanted you to become a detective.\" [1641] \"This case can only make me a pitiful, useless man,\" I said, revealing my true feelings for the first time, \"and that's the most maddening thing.\" [1642] Suddenly, I heard a sound, like someone knocking on the door of the living room, or not. \"Who is it?\" I shouted. [1643] \"It's me,\" said Blanche Enderby's voice from the hall, sounding somewhat anxious, \"gentlemen, I'm sorry to disturb you at this time, but a lady is looking for M. Poirot. She said she couldn't wait.\" [1644] \"Please let her in, madam.\" [1645] A few seconds later, I found myself in the presence of the artist Nancy Drew. I felt that most men would find her alluringly beautiful. [1646] Poirot introduced us politely. [1647] \"Thank you for seeing me,\" Nancy Drew's eyes were puffy, indicating that she had been crying, and her expensive-looking dark green coat looked expensive, \"I feel very presumptuous to disturb you like this, but...you see, I still came. I tried to convince myself not to come, but...\"\n[1648] \"Please sit down, Mrs. Drew,\" Poirot said, \"How did you find us?\" [1649] \"Like a proper detective, I found out where you lived from Scotland Yard,\" Nancy managed a slight smile. [1650] \"Oh! Poirot, he thought he had chosen a place where no one could find him, but the police brought the guests to the door! No matter, madam, I'm glad to see you, just a little surprised.\" [1651] \"I want to tell you what happened sixteen years ago in Glebeholme,\" said Miss Duane. \"I should have told you earlier, but when you mentioned those names I never wanted to hear again, I was really scared.\" [1652] She unbuttoned her coat and took it off, gesturing for her to sit down. [1653] After sitting down, she said, \"It's a sad story.\" [1654] Miss Duane spoke in a low voice, her eyes betraying her nervousness. The story she told us was exactly the same as Margaret Ernst had told me in Glebeholme, about how Patrick Ives was cruelly defamed. When she mentioned Jenny Hobbs, her voice even trembled. \"She was the worst. You see, she fell in love with Patrick. Oh, there was no evidence, but that's how I think it. What she did to him showed her love for him: because of jealousy, she fabricated an unforgivable lie. He loved me, so she wanted to hurt him, punish him. Then, when Harriet caught her in the lie, Jenny realized how much damage she had caused and felt sorry. I believe she must have been terribly ashamed and hated herself, but she didn't take any steps to make amends, she didn't do anything! She ran away, hid herself, hoping no one would ever find her. Even though she was so afraid of Harriet, she should have been brave enough to stand up and say, 'I told a lie, I'm sorry.'\" [1655] \"I'm sorry, madam. You said there was no evidence that Jenny loved Patrick Ives, so I would like to ask, how do you know she loved him? As you said, a person who loves him would never defame him like that, it's hard to believe.\" [1656] \"I just believe she loved Patrick without a doubt,\" Nancy insisted. \"She left her lover in Cambridge and moved to Gresham Holling with Patrick and Francis. Do you know about that?\" [1657] We all shook our heads. [1658] \"They were supposed to get married. I believe they even had a date set, but Jenny couldn't let go of Patrick, so she canceled the wedding and left with him.\" [1659] \"Perhaps the one she couldn't let go of was Francis Yve, or the Yves themselves? She might have valued loyalty over romantic love.\" [1660] \"I don't believe any woman would be so loyal to her employer that she would give up her own marriage,\" said Nancy. [1661] \"I don't believe it either, madam. But what you say is not very logical. If Jenny was jealous of love, why did she only tell this lie when Patrick Yve fell in love with you? Why didn't she feel jealous when he married Francis?\" [1662] \"How do you know she wasn't jealous? Patrick and Francis met and got married in Cambridge. Jenny Hobbs served him then. Maybe she whispered some nasty things in his friend's ear, but that friend wasn't Harriet Sipper, and the mean words didn't spread.\" [1663] Poirot nodded, saying, \"You are right, there is a possibility.\" [1664] \"Most people don't like to spread bad rumors, thank goodness,\" Nancy said, \"In Cambridge, there might not have been anyone as malicious as Harriet Sipper, and no one as zealous in promoting morality and piety as Ada Gransbury.\" [1665] \"I noticed you didn't mention Richard Nigg.\" [1666] Nancy looked somewhat confused. \"Richard is a good man. He regretted what he did in this terrible affair later. Oh, when he found out it was Jenny's vile lie, he was deeply sorry and stopped sympathizing with Ada. A few years ago, he wrote me a letter from Devonshire, saying this matter had been tormenting his heart. He said Patrick and I shouldn't have done what we did, and he always believed that marriage vows were marriage vows, but he began to think that punishment was not always the right way to solve problems, even if someone had done wrong.\" [1667] \"He said that in his letter?\" [1668] \"Yes. I suppose you don't agree.\" [1669] \"These matters are complicated, madam.\" [1670] \"For instance, is it right to commit a greater sin to punish someone for loving the wrong person, and to cause two deaths, one of which was innocent?\" [1671] \"Yes, that's exactly the kind of dilemma that makes things so complicated.\" [1672] \"He also wrote in the letter that, as a Christian, he didn't believe God would want him to persecute someone as naturally good as Patrick.\" [1673] \"Punishment and persecution are two different concepts.\" Poirot said, \"There's another question: was there any illegal activity involved in the whole affair? Falling in love...after all, we can't control our feelings, but we can choose whether to act on them.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe plan she described was the same as the one she had told me and Poirot about at Samuel Kid's house, including how things had gone awry later. When she mentioned that they would frame Nancy Duane with the murders, ensuring she would be hanged, Louisa Wallace let out a shriek. \"Framing an innocent woman, putting her to death for three murders she did not commit, is not correcting the mistake! This is evil!\" exclaimed St. John Wallace. [2172] No one contradicted him, at least no one spoke out against him. I noticed that Phyllis Spurling did not seem as shocked as most people, and she appeared to be listening intently. [2173] \"I never intended to frame Nancy,\" said Jenny. \"Never! Whether you believe me or not, it doesn't matter.\" [2174] \"Mr. Niggles,\" said Poirot, \"Henry Niggles, do you think your brother Richard would devise such a plan?\" [2175] Henry Niggles stood up and said, \"I don't think so, Mr. Poirot. The Richard I know would never think of killing anyone, of course, the Richard who moved to Devonshire and lived with me sixteen years ago is not the same as the Richard I know. Oh, they look alike on the outside, but they are completely different on the inside. I'm afraid I never really understood the one who came after, so I can't really comment on whether he might have done such a thing.\" [2176] \"Thank you, Mr. Nigges. And thank you, Miss Hobbs.\" He added coldly, \"Please sit down.\" [2177] He turned to the crowd and said, \"Ladies and gentlemen, you see, if Miss Hobbs' words are true, we don't need to arrest anyone, no one is guilty. Adah Gransberry killed her with the permission of Harriet Sipper, Richard Nigges killed her with the permission of Adah Gransberry, and then, since Jenny Hobbs did not go to the hotel as planned, Richard Nigges committed suicide. He locked the door, hid the key behind a loose tile in the fireplace, opened the window, and ended his life, making the scene look like a murder. The police would think that the killer, Nancy Drew, escaped with the key, using the open window and the tree by the window. But according to Jenny Hobbs, there was no killer, everyone died willingly!\" [2178] Poirot looked around the dining room. \"There is no killer,\" he repeated. \"Nevertheless, there are still two living criminals who should be punished. They are Jenny Hobbs and Samuel Kid, who conspired to frame Nancy Drew.\" [2179] \"I hope you lock them up, Mr. Poirot!\" said Louisa Wallace loudly. [2180] \"I don't have the authority to open or close the prison gates, madam. That's the work of my friend Cader and his colleagues. I'm just responsible for uncovering the hidden and the truth. Mr. Samuel Kid, please stand up.\" [2181] Kid stood up, looking uncomfortable. [2182] \"Your task in this plan was to place a note at the hotel's reception desk, was it not? The note read, 'May they rest in peace, 121, 238, 317'?\" [2183] \"Yes, sir. Just as Miss Jennie said, that's exactly what it was.\" [2184] \"Did Miss Jennie give you the note?\" [2185] \"Yes, she gave it to me earlier that day. That morning.\" [2186] \"When did you put it on the table?\" [2187] \"Shortly after eight o'clock in the evening, just like Jenny said. The sooner after eight, the better. But first, make sure no one is around, no one will see me.\" [2188] \"Who said that?\" Polo asked. [2189] \"Jenny.\" [2190] \"Is it Jenny who told you to put the key in Nancy Duane's pocket?\" [2191] \"Yes.\" Kid spoke with annoyance. \"She just said it, and I don't understand why you're asking me again.\" [2192] \"Alright, let me explain. According to Jenny Hobson's original plan, Jenny was supposed to give all three room keys to Samuel Kid after killing Richard, and Samuel would then find a way to plant the keys in a place that would frame Nancy Drew - which, as it turned out, was in Nancy Drew's coat pocket. However, Jenny Hobson said that she didn't go to the Blaukhan Hotel that night. She didn't have the courage to do so. So, I'd like to ask you, Mr. Kid, how did you get the keys to rooms 121 and 317?\" [2193] \"How did I... how did I get those keys?\" [2194] \"Yes, that's my question. Please answer.\" [2195] \"I... Well, if you must know, I got the keys through my own cleverness. I spoke nicely to an employee at the hotel and asked if he could do me a favor and let me use the master key, and he agreed. I returned the key after I was done, and the whole process was very discreet.\" [2196] I was close to Poirot and could hear him make an annoyed sound. He said, \"Which employee, sir? All the employees are here. Point out the one who gave you the master key.\" [2197] \"I can't remember who it was. It was a man. I can't remember people's faces.\" [2198] \"So, you used the master key to enter three rooms?\" [2199] \"No, I only went to room 238. All the keys should have been there, waiting for Jenny to come and take them, but I only found two. As you said, there's another one hidden behind a loose tile in the fireplace, but Mr. Nigel's body was lying there, and I didn't want to stay there too long, so I didn't look for the third key in the room.\" [2200] \"You're lying,\" Poirot said to him. \"It doesn't matter. You'll find that lying won't help you get out of this predicament. Let's continue. No, don't sit down yet. I have another question for you and Jenny Hobbs. You two planned that, after 7:30 p.m. on the night of the murder, Jenny would come to the 'Joyful Coffee House' and tell me the story she had prepared about how scared she was of being murdered, right?\" [2201] \"Yes.\" Jenny answered, looking at Samuel Kid instead of Poirot. [2202] \"I'm sorry, I still have an important question that I haven't figured out. You said you were too scared to carry out the plan, so you didn't go to the hotel at 6 p.m. But it seems that the plan went smoothly without you, the only difference being that Richard Nigges committed suicide, not by you putting poison in his drink. Lady, am I right?\" [2203] \"Yes, that's right.\" [2204] \"So, the only detail that changed was that Richard Nigges committed suicide, so we can say that the death still occurred according to plan: between 7:15 p.m. and 8:00 p.m., after ordering sandwiches and scones. Right, Ms. Hobbs?\" [2205] \"Yes.\" Jenny said, but her voice didn't sound as confident as before. [2206] \"So, I would like to ask, how could you possibly carry out the plan to kill Richard Nigges? You told us that you planned to meet me at 'The Happy Cafe' after seven-thirty, knowing that I always go there for dinner on Thursdays. But from the Bluxton Hotel to 'The Happy Cafe', it takes at least half an hour, no matter what mode of transportation you use. So, even if you killed Adah Gransbury quickly at seven-fifteen and Richard Nigges quickly killed Adah Gransbury, you still wouldn't have been able to be at the 238 room to kill Richard Nigges and then make it to the cafe by seven-thirty. Do you want us to believe that, in all your careful planning of this action, you didn't consider that it was actually impossible?\" [2207] Jenny's face went pale, and I felt mine do the same, even though I couldn't see it. [2208] Poirot's pointed out flaw was so obvious that I hadn't noticed it. I hadn't even thought of it. [2209] Chapter XXIII\n[2210] The True Adah Gransbury\n[2211] Samuel Kidd chuckled softly and turned to face the others in the restaurant. \"Mr. Poirot, you take pride in your investigative abilities, but it seems your powers of observation are not as sharp as you claim. I can say with confidence that I am more familiar with this plan than you are, as Jane has mentioned it countless times. The original plan was not to start at seven fifteen, but at six o'clock. I don't know where these ideas came from. Asking for food at seven fifteen was not part of the plan.\" [2212] \"Yes,\" Jane said, her sharp mind finding a way out of the trap that seemed to have restored her composure. \"I can only say that I was late to the hotel at six o'clock, which caused the plan to be delayed. Others may have wanted to discuss why I didn't show up. I should have been there by then. The discussion would have taken some time.\" [2213] \"Ah, of course. But a few minutes ago, when I asked you if the deaths were carried out according to the agreed plan, between seven fifteen and eight o'clock, you didn't correct me. And neither of you mentioned that the afternoon tea was not part of the plan.\" [2214] \"I apologize for not correcting your statement earlier,\" Jane said. \"I... I mean, it's so oppressive here.\" [2215] \"So, the three executions were scheduled to begin at six o'clock, then?\" [2216] \"Yes, everything was supposed to be completed by 6:45 so that I could arrive at 'The Happy Coffee Shop' at 7:30.\" [2217] \"Then I have another question for you, madam. Why, in your plan, was Mr. Kid supposed to wait an hour before placing the note on the hotel's front desk? By then, Harriet, Ada, and Richard were already dead, and you had left the hotel. Why not at another time, such as 7:15 or 7:30? Why choose 8:00?\" [2218] Jenny recoiled as if struck on the head. \"Why not 8:00?\" she challenged. \"Waiting a little longer won't hurt. [2219] \"Your question is ridiculous, Mr. Poirot,\" said Samuel Kid. [2220] \"Waiting a little longer won't hurt, madam. I agree. So we must ask, what was the purpose of leaving the note? Why not wait until the next morning, when the hotel staff would naturally discover the three bodies? Jenny, don't look at Samuel Kid, look at me, Hercule Poirot! Answer my question.\" [2221] \"I...I don't know! I think maybe Richard...\"\n[2222] \"No! Not Richard!\" Poirot said to her. \"If you don't want to answer my question, I'll answer it for you. You made Mr. Kid put the note on the front desk just after 8:00 because the murder was planned to be done between 7:15 and 8:00!\" [2223] Poirot turned around and faced the crowd with widened eyes, listening quietly. \"Let's think about the afternoon tea that the three victims ordered and delivered to Room 317, Ada Grantham's room. Let's imagine that the three people who willingly sought death were confused because Jane didn't show up. They didn't know what to do, so they went to Ada Grantham's room to discuss it. Mr. Cargill, would you order scones and cakes if you were about to be executed for your past sins?\" [2224] \"No, I would be too nervous to eat or drink.\" [2225] \"Perhaps our three executioners thought they needed to conserve their energy for the upcoming task,\" Poirot speculated. \"However, when the food arrived, they couldn't eat it. But where did the food go?\" [2226] \"Are you asking me?\" Jennie said, \"I don't know, because I wasn't there.\" [2227] \"Let's go back to the time of the murder,\" Poirot said. \"The pathologist determined that the three deaths occurred between four and eight-thirty. Later, indirect evidence narrowed the time of death to seven-fifteen to eight-ten. Alright, let's look at these indirect evidence. Server Rafael Bocar was in Room 317 at seven-fifteen and saw the three victims, and then Thomas Braynell met Richard Nigges in the hotel lobby at seven-thirty, where Nigges praised Braynell's efficiency and asked him to ensure the tea point charges were on his account, and ordered a sherry. So, none of the cases happened before seven-fifteen, and Richard Nigges couldn't have died before seven-thirty. [2228] \"However, there are still many details that don't fit together, and we can't form a complete picture. First, we know that Harriet Sipper, Adela Granbriery, and Richard Nigges didn't eat those pastries, but they disappeared. I don't believe that a person who is about to commit their first murder would want to eat a scone before doing so. Since they didn't plan to eat, why did they order them? It can only be to create an alibi, to prove that they were still alive at seven-fifteen. But why make people think that the three victims were still alive at that time? According to Jennie Hobbs, I can only think of one explanation: if our conspirators knew, for whatever reason, that Nancy Duane had no alibi between seven-fifteen and eight-fifteen, then they had to ensure that the deaths occurred within that time frame. But Nancy Duane's alibi was very solid, wasn't it, Mrs. Wallace?\" [2229] Louisa Wallace stood up and said, \"Yes, she had one. She ate dinner at my house, and then stayed with me and my husband until about ten o'clock in the evening.\" [2230] \"Thank you, ma'am. So, why did they have to make it look like the murders happened between seven-fifteen and eight-ten, I can only think of one reason. That is, during that time, Jennie Hobbs also had an unbreakable alibi. That is, I, Hercule Poirot, can perfectly prove that she was not at the Brooks Han Hotel at that time. She was with me from seven-thirty-five to seven-fifty in 'Joy Cafe'. Additionally, I have already mentioned the distance between the two places.\" [2231] \"I put these evidences together, along with my belief that the three people did not die between 7:15 PM and 8:10 PM. So I thought, why go to such lengths to prove that Jane did not commit the murder? Unless, she really did it.\" [2232] Jane jumped up from her chair, saying, \"I didn't kill them! I swear I didn't! And they must have died between 7:15 PM and 8:00 PM, everyone here knows it except you!\" [2233] \"Sit down and shut up, Miss Hobbs,\" said Poirot coldly. [2234] Samuel Kidd's face was full of anger. \"These are all made up, Mr. Poirot! How do you know they didn't order the food because they were hungry?\" [2235] \"Well, Mr. Kidd, why didn't they eat them?\" I asked. \"Where did the sandwiches and cakes go?\" [2236] \"That was the best afternoon tea in London!\" murmured Luca Lazari. [2237] \"I'll tell you where they went, Caderpohl,\" said Poirot. \"Our murderer made a mistake - just one of many. If there had been food on the plate in Room 317, it would have been fine. The police would have thought that the killer came in before they had a chance to enjoy their snacks. But the killer was afraid that leaving food would arouse suspicion, he didn't want anyone to ask, 'Why didn't you eat the tea and cake?'\" [2238] \"What happened to the food?\" I asked. \"Where did it go?\" [2239] \"The conspirators took the food away from the scene. Yes, ladies and gentlemen, there was a plot and three murders were committed! There's one more thing I haven't explained - Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nigges were already dead before 7:15.\" [2240] Hearing this, Luca Lazari stepped forward and said, \"Mr. Poirot, I must interrupt you. I must tell you that Raphael Bukar is our most loyal employee and he would not lie. He saw the three victims alive and well when he went to deliver the food at 7:15. Alive and well! You must be mistaken.\" [2241] \"I didn't. But one thing you're right about, Raffaello Puccini is a good witness. He saw three people in Room 317 when he delivered the tea, but they weren't Harriet Spear, Edith Gransbury, and Richard Nix.\" [2242] Everyone in the restaurant gasped in shock, and I was no exception, trying to figure out who the three people could be. Not Janice Hobbs, who was on her way to \"The Happy Cafe.\" Who could it be? [2243] \"Poirot,\" I said nervously, \"You mean there were three people dressed up as the victims just to make the waiter think they were still alive when he delivered the tea?\" [2244] \"No, that's not quite it. In fact, two people dressed up as two of the victims. The third person, Edith Gransbury... unfortunately, she wasn't a fake. No, unfortunately, she was the real Edith Gransbury. Mr. Puccini, do you remember how you described what you saw when you delivered the tea to Room 317? You told me twice, and I remember every word. For everyone's sake, don't mind if I repeat it?\" [2245] \"No, sir, I don't mind.\" [2246] \"Thank you. You found the three victims alive - of course, they were talking about a mutual acquaintance. You heard Harriet Sipple, or the woman referred to as 'Harriet' in the room say, 'She has no other choice, does she? He no longer trusts her. He doesn't like her now, and it's her own choice to go there, and she's old enough to be his mother. No, if she wants to know what he's thinking, she has no choice but to accept the woman he trusts and talk to her.' At that point, the man in the room ignored you and the food and said, 'Oh, Harriet, that's not fair. Ada can be easily frightened, be kind to her.' Do I have it right, Mr. Burbank?\" [2247] \"Yes, sir.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[255] I suddenly became impatient and decided to speak my mind: \"I hope you don't think I'm a fool too. I said there were many guests who checked in on Wednesday... I was too hasty in saying that. The guests who checked in on Wednesday but did not die the following day have nothing to do with it, right? My point is, only when three or more seemingly unrelated guests check in on the same day and are murdered on the same night can it be considered a significant coincidence.\" [256] \"Yes.\" Poirot said with a sincere smile as he stepped into the elevator, \"You have finally regained your sharp mind. My judgment was not mistaken. You said 'seemingly unrelated' and you hit the nail on the head. Now I can assure you that these three victims must have had a connection. They were not randomly selected from the hotel guests. The reason for their deaths is the same - all related to the initials PIJ. And the reason they checked in on the same day at the same hotel is the same.\" [257] \"Like an invitation to come and die.\" I said casually, \"The invitation said, 'Please arrive a day in advance so that you can enjoy death on Thursday.'\" [258] Joking about this may not be appropriate, but when I'm feeling down, I can only make jokes. Sometimes I can even fool myself into thinking everything is still okay. However, this time it didn't work. [259] \"Enjoy... \" Poirot muttered, \"Yes, my friend, that's exactly what I mean. I know you were just joking, but you hit the nail on the head and it's quite interesting.\" [260] I don't agree, it was just a stupid joke with no other meaning. But Poirot seemed to genuinely appreciate my absurd idea. [261] \"One, two, three,\" Poirot murmured as the elevator ascended. \"Harriet Spear stays on the 121, Richard Niggles on the 238, and Ada Gransbury on the 317. These three victims are on consecutive floors. It's quite organized, isn't it?\" Poirot usually appreciates order, but this time, the organized arrangement seemed to unsettle him. [262] We inspected three nearly identical rooms. Each had a bed, a wardrobe, a washbasin, a glass cup facing upwards on the sink, several armchairs, a small coffee table, a desk, a tiled fireplace, a radiator, a large desk in front of the window, a suitcase, some clothes, and personal items, as well as a corpse. [263] All the doors slammed shut, trapping me inside\u2026\n[264] \"Grab his hand, Edward.\" [265] I dared not get too close to the bodies. They lay rigidly on the ground, feet facing the door, arms flat on their sides. Neatly arranged. [266] (Just writing these sentences describing the corpse's posture was already unbearable. It's no wonder I couldn't bear to look at the three corpses up close, only for a few seconds at a time? The bluish skin, the stiff, swollen tongue, the wrinkled lips. Indeed, I shouldn't have focused on the lifeless hands, but rather examined their faces closely; I shouldn't have been lost in thought, but I couldn't help it: I wondered if Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nigg would have wanted someone to hold their hands after death, or if they would have been horrified by the idea. Goodness, the human brain is a fickle and uncontrollable organ. Just thinking about it made me feel miserable.) [267] Neatly\u2026\n[268] Suddenly, an idea seized me. The crime scenes were too strange. I realized that the way the bodies were arranged resembled the way doctors position patients who have been treated for months but ultimately die. The bodies of Harriet Sipper, Ada Gransbury, and Richard Nigg had been meticulously arranged - at least, that's how it seemed to me. The killer had taken care of them after the murder, which suggested that he was cold-blooded and chilling. [269] Just as I had this thought, I dismissed it. This place doesn't provide such services. I had mixed past and present, mixing what happened at the Blakeshan Hotel with my most unpleasant childhood memories. I commanded myself not to be distracted and focused on the matter at hand, trying to see the facts through Pollock's eyes without the distortion of my own life experiences. [270] Each victim lay on the floor between a wingback armchair and a small coffee table. In Harriet Sipper and Ada Gransbury's rooms, there was a teacup with a saucer on the small coffee table; in Richard Nigg's room, there was only a sherry glass. In Ada Gransbury's 317 room, there was a tray on the larger table by the window, with a set of teacups and saucers, an empty teacup, and a plate with some crumbs, but nothing else. [271] \"Ah ha,\" said Poirot, \"there are two tea cups and many plates in this room, and it's clear that someone had dinner with Miss Ada Gransberry. It's probably the killer. But why is the tray still in the room? When were the trays taken away from the rooms of Harriet Sipper and Richard Nigges?\" [272] \"They might not have ordered anything,\" I said. \"They might have just ordered drinks - tea and sherry - so there was no tray to begin with. And Ada Gransberry brought a lot of clothes, twice as many as the other two. Look, there's not even room for another petticoat in the wardrobe.\" [273] \"You're right,\" said Poirot. \"Lazari said they all ordered dinner, and we'll check later what each of them specifically ordered. I wouldn't have overlooked this if I hadn't been worried about Jenny. I still don't know where Jenny is! And Jenny is about the same age as these three victims, between forty and forty-five years old.\" [274] While Poirot checked their mouths and cufflinks, I turned my face away. He checked them, occasionally exclaiming in various ways. I looked at the fireplace and out the window from time to time, trying to avoid thinking again about the hands that would never have to be held again. I even thought about my crossword puzzle, where I had made a mistake. [275] After checking the three rooms, Poirot insisted on returning to the third floor, room 238, where Richard Nigges was. Perhaps going to the rooms of the victims so many times won't make me feel uncomfortable? So far, the answer is still no. As I entered Nigges' room again, it felt like I was being forced to climb a treacherous mountain, and I knew beforehand that reaching the summit would lead to disaster. [276] Poirot did not notice my distress, and I hoped it was because I had covered it up and not attracted his attention. He stood in the middle of the room and said to me, \"My friend, this room is the most different, isn't it? Indeed, Miss Edith Garnet's room has an extra teacup and tray, but here there is a sherry glass, no teacup, and a window that is completely open, while the windows in the other two rooms are closed. It's cold in Mr. Nix's room!\" [277] \"Lazari found the body in this room just like this,\" I said, \"without any changes.\" [278] Poirot walked to the open window and said, \"This is the beautiful view that Lazari wanted to show me, the hotel garden. Miss Harriet Sippel and Miss Edith Garnet's rooms are on the other side of the hotel, where you can see 'Great London.' Can you see those trees, Kattimer?\" [279] I told him I had seen them, wondering if he thought I was an idiot for not seeing the trees right by the window. [280] \"Another difference in this room is the position of the cufflinks,\" said Poirot. \"Did you notice? Miss Harriet Sippel and Miss Edith Garnet's cufflinks were in their mouths, slightly protruding from their lips. While Richard Nix's cufflinks were very deep inside, almost at his throat.\" [281] I opened my mouth to object, then gave up, but it was too late, Poirot saw the denial in my eyes. So he asked, \"What do you want to say?\" [282] \"I think you're being too rigid,\" I said. \"The three victims all had a cufflink with the initials PIJ in their mouths, which is a common point, not a different one. The position of the cufflink in the mouth is not a big issue.\" [283] \"This is a huge difference! The lips and throat are different positions, completely different!\" Poirot came over to me and stood in front of me, continuing, \"Katchuk, please remember this: when three murders are almost identical, every tiny detail is crucial.\" [284] Do I really have to remember this pearl of wisdom, even though I don't agree with it? However, Poirot need not worry, I remember everything he has said to me, word for word, and the more he angers me, the better I remember. [285] \"All three victims had cufflinks in their mouths,\" I repeated stubbornly, \"and that's enough for me.\" [286] \"I understand,\" Poirot said with disappointment. \"It's enough for you, and it's probably enough for the hundreds of people under you, and I have no doubt it's enough for your superiors and Scotland Yard. But for Hercule Poirot, it's not enough!\" [287] I had to remind myself that Poirot was talking about the similarities and differences in the case, not me. [288] \"What about the open windows?\" he asked me. \"The windows in the other two rooms were closed. Is that not important?\" [289] \"Maybe it doesn't matter,\" I said. \"Richard Nigges might have opened this room's window himself, and there was no reason for the killer to close it. Poirot, you often say that even in the winter, British people like to open windows because we believe it's good for the body.\" [290] \"My friend,\" Poirot said patiently, \"consider this: it is impossible for the three people not to have been poisoned, to have fallen naturally from the armchair and lie flat on the floor with their hands by their sides and their feet pointing towards the door. Why didn't anyone crawl across the room? Why didn't anyone fall on the other side of the chair? The killer rearranged their bodies to ensure that each corpse was in the same position, at the same distance from the chair and the small table. So, since the killer went to such lengths to make the three crime scenes identical, why didn't he close this room's window too? Perhaps Richard Nigges opened the window himself, but why didn't the killer close it to keep it consistent with the other two rooms' windows?\" [291] I had to think about this. Poirot was right; the bodies had been arranged intentionally to make them look identical. [292] The bodies arranged neatly...\n[293] \"I think it depends on where you draw the line for the crime scene,\" I said, trying to pull myself back to the darkest room I had ever experienced. \"It depends on whether you include the window or not.\" [294] \"Draw the line for the crime scene?\" [295] \"Yes, not literally, but theoretically. Perhaps, the killer only created a small crime scene here.\" [296] Poirot chuckles, trying to hide his smile with his small mustache. \"A theoretically defined crime scene. Ah, I see. Where does the crime scene begin and where does it end? That's indeed a question. Can it be smaller than this room? It's an interesting question for philosophers.\" [297] \"Thank you.\" [298] \"You're welcome. Carstairs, tell me, what do you think happened at the Blaxland Hotel last night? Let's put aside the motive for a moment and talk about what you think the killer did? First, second, third, like that.\" [299] \"I don't know.\" [300] \"Think carefully, Carstairs.\" [301] \"Hmm... I think he came to the hotel, pocketed the cufflinks, and went to these three rooms in order. He probably went to Room 317 first, Ada Grantsberry's room, and then went downstairs, so he could leave the hotel quickly after killing the last person \u2013 Harryette Sipper in Room 121 on the second floor. If he went down one more floor, he could easily escape.\" [302] \"So, what did he do in these three rooms?\" [303] I sighed and said, \"You already know the answer. He killed them, arranged the bodies neatly, put a cufflink in each mouth, locked the doors, and left.\" [304] \"Did he encounter any obstacles when entering each room? Did each victim have a drink prepared for him, waiting to be poisoned \u2013 and were the drinks delivered at 7:15 by the hotel staff? Did he stand by the victim's side, watching them drink the poison, and then stand there waiting for them to die? Did he have dinner with one of them, and did Ada Grantsberry order him a cup of tea? All these actions \u2013 going to three rooms, killing three people, putting cufflinks in their mouths, arranging the bodies in a straight line with their feet facing the door \u2013 could all be done in the time between 7:15 and 8:10? My dear friend, it's impossible, completely impossible.\" [305] \"You're right. Poirot, do you have a more reasonable explanation? After all, that's why you're here \u2013 to find a more reasonable explanation. You can start explaining whenever you want.\" As soon as I said these words, I regretted it, feeling too impulsive. [306] \"I've already started.\" Hercule said, thankfully not sounding angry. \"You said the killer left a note on the front desk saying he killed someone. Show it to me.\" [307] I took the note out of my pocket and handed it to him. John Good, the model employee mentioned by Lazare, found the note on the front desk at 8:20 last night. It read, \"May they rest in peace, 121, 238, 317.\" [308] \"This killer, or accomplice, had the audacity to approach the hotel's front desk, where he could have been seen by witnesses with the note in his hand. He was so bold and confident that he didn't sneak out through the back door.\" Hercule said. \"He was really daring and confident.\" [309] \"After Lazare saw the note, he immediately checked these three rooms and found their bodies. He even boasted to me that he checked all the rooms in the hotel afterwards. Luckily, all the other guests were unharmed.\" I said, although I knew I shouldn't say such unprofessional things. If Hercule were British, I would try to control my emotions better. [310] \"I know I shouldn't say this, but I feel better after saying it. If Hercule were British, I might try to control my emotions better.\" [311] \"Mr. Lazari would think the killer is among the guests, right? No, he wouldn't, the guests staying at the Brawnsham Hotel are all of high moral character and integrity!\" [312] I coughed and tilted my head towards the door. Poirot turned around and saw Lazari standing at the door. He looked very happy and said, \"Yes, that's right, Poirot sir.\" [313] \"Everyone who was in the hotel on Thursday must come to Mr. Kitchbull's place to explain their whereabouts,\" Poirot said sternly to him. \"Everyone, every guest, every employee, everyone who was in the hotel that day.\" [314] \"I'm at your service, Mr. Kitchbull, you can talk to whoever you want,\" Lazari said respectfully to me. \"Once we clean up the leftovers from breakfast, the restaurant will be yours to use. Feel free to have them all gathered there.\" [315] \"Thank you. And I'll check those three rooms thoroughly again,\" said Poirot. I was surprised, as they had already been checked. \"Kitchbull, find out where Harryt Westphal, Ada Grantsberry, and Richard Nigges live; which hotel employee booked their rooms; what food and drinks they ordered; and who delivered them to their rooms when.\" [316] I got up to leave, afraid he would keep giving me more tasks. [317] I had gone, he called after me, \"See if there is a woman named Jeanne in the hotel, or working here.\" [318] \"There is no Jeanne in the Bluxton Hotel, Mr. Poirot,\" Lazari said. \"You should ask me, not Mr. Katchadourian. I know everyone here, the Bluxton Hotel is a happy family!\" [319] * * *\n[320] [1] In Britain, the first floor refers to the second floor. Therefore, Miss Harry Westbury's room, number 121, is actually on the second floor. In all instances where floors and room numbers are mentioned, the first number is increased by one to indicate the floor on which the room is located. [321] Chapter 4\n[322] The crime scene expands\n[323] Sometimes, recalling something someone said months or years ago can still make you laugh. For me, it was what Poirot said to me later that afternoon. He said, \"Even the most clever detective would be at a loss when facing Mr. Lazari. If you don't praise him enough, he'll add on his own; as long as you keep flattering him, he'll patiently listen.\" [324] But Poirot finally succeeded in convincing Lazari to leave and let him check Room 238 alone. Lazari left without closing the door, and Poirot went over to close it and breathed a sigh of relief. It was much easier to think clearly without anyone else talking. [325] He walked straight to the window and stared out, pondering. Perhaps the killer had opened the window to escape, climbing down the tree after killing Nigus. [326] Why escape in this way? Why not exit normally through the corridor? Maybe the killer heard someone talking outside Nigel's room and didn't want to take the risk of being seen when leaving? Yes, that's possible. But when he casually walked to the front desk, placing the note on the table and claiming to have killed three people, why wasn't he afraid of being seen? Doing so would not only be seen but also potentially caught with evidence that could convict him on the spot. [327] Poirot looked at the corpse on the floor again, with no metallic glint on his lips. Out of the three victims, only Richard Nigel's cufflinks were on his throat, which was a bit unusual. There were many unusual things about this room. Therefore, Poirot decided to re-examine room 238 first. He... no need to deny it, he indeed suspected this room. Out of the three rooms, he liked this one the least. There was some disorder here, out of line. [328] Poirot stood by Nigel's corpse, frowning. Even by his standards, an open window did not suffice to say that the room was disordered. What made him feel this way? He slowly turned around, taking another look at the room. No, he must have been mistaken.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:45:39 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIf you ask a doctor or a medical expert, theoretically, can a twelve or thirteen-year-old girl have a child? The answer is either 'yes' or 'no.' Let's not get caught up in the age at which a woman can have a child! Remember Samuel Kid's comment about the young man, it was quite amusing. He said, 'Does he have a brain? I don't think he does.' You would think that Kid's meaning was that the young man was a fool.\" [2441] \"Yes, that's exactly what I think,\" I said impatiently. \"Since you're so much smarter than me, why don't you just tell me where I'm wrong?\" [2442] Polo grumbled discontentedly. \"My goodness! The couple in Room 317 were Harriet Sipper and her husband George. It wasn't a serious debate, it was just joking. George Sipper died when they were both young, and Samuel Kid said he didn't have a brain, meaning that if he existed after death, he wouldn't be in human form, but in a soul, right? Souls don't have human brains, so George Sipper's soul couldn't have a brain either.\" [2443] \"I\u2026 oh, my goodness. Yes, now I understand.\" [2444] \"Samuel Kid began his argument with 'I think\u2026' because he knew that Nancy Duane would disagree with him. She might say, 'Souls certainly have ideas, and souls can be attached to people, don't they have free will? Where does their will come from if they don't have a brain?'\" [2445] From a philosophical perspective, this is an interesting point of view. In other circumstances, I might also express my own opinion.\" [2446] Poirot continued, \"Nancy said 'old enough to be his mother' because she believed that when a person dies, their age is fixed forever. In the afterlife, they won't age. If George Sipper's soul could come back to visit his wife, he would still be a twenty-something-year-old, the same age he was when he died. And she, as a forty-something-year-old woman, is now old enough to be his mother.\" [2447] \"Great!\" Jenny exclaimed sincerely, \"I wasn't there at the time, but they argued again in front of me later. Mr. Poirot, you are truly insightful. Mr. Kitchener, I hope you appreciate him.\" She then turned to Poirot and said, \"Their argument never ends...oh, forever! Nancy insists she's right, and Sem doesn't back down. He says souls are not limited by time dimensions, they don't have a concept of time, so you can't say someone is old enough to be a soul's mother.\" [2448] Poirot said to me, \"How disgusting, isn't it, Mr. Kitchener? When Rafael Buccar went to deliver the dessert, Nancy Drew sat next to the corpse of Ida Gransberry propped up on a chair, mocking another woman who died that day due to their conspiracy. Poor and foolish Harryette, her husband had no interest in communicating with her directly from the grave, he only wanted to talk to Jenny Hobbs. And Harryette had no choice, if she wanted to receive his message, she had to go to Bluxton to see Jenny. But going to see Jenny meant certain death.\" [2449] \"No one deserves to be killed more than Harryette Sipper.\" Jenny said, \"I regret many things, but I don't regret killing Harryette.\" [2450] \"What about Ida Gransberry?\" I asked, \"Why did she go to the Bluxton Hotel?\" [2451] \"Ah!\" Poirot said, always eager to share his seemingly endless knowledge with others, \"Ada also received an irresistible invitation from Richard Nigges. Not to speak with the deceased lover, but to meet her separated for sixteen years' ex-fianc\u00e9. It's not hard to imagine how tempting that is. Richard Nigges abandoned Ada, which undoubtedly broke her heart. She remained single for life. I think he hinted at reconciliation, even possibly marriage, in his letter. Ada agreed \u2013 who wouldn't give love another chance? Richard told her he would come to her room at Bluxton Hotel on Thursday from 3:30 to 4:00 pm. Ketteral, do you remember saying that you would go to the hotel on Wednesday so that you could spend Thursday preparing for the murder? Does it make more sense now?\" [2452] I nodded and said, \"Nigges knew he was going to commit murder on Thursday and that he would be killed himself. To prepare mentally for the double test, he naturally wanted to be on the scene a day earlier.\" [2453] \"Also to avoid any delays that could hinder the plan,\" Poirot said. [2454] \"So, Jane Hobbs killed Harriet Seppel, and Richard Nigges killed Ada Gransberry?\" I asked. [2455] \"Yes, my friend,\" Poirot said, looking at Jane, who nodded. \"I believe the murders in rooms 121 and 317 were carried out around the same time. I suspect the methods were the same, luring Harriet and Ada to drink poison. Jane told Harriet, 'I think you need to drink some water before I tell you what I have to say. Sit down, and I'll get you some water.' They took cups from the sink to fill with water, and they slipped in the poison. Then they handed the poisoned water to the victims, who soon died.\" [2456] \"How did Richard Naygreaves die?\" I asked. [2457] \"According to their previous plan, Jeanne was supposed to kill him,\" said Poirot. [2458] \"Most of what I said to you at Semple's was true,\" said Jeanne. \"Richard did write to me after many years. He was tormented by what he had done to Patrick and Francis. He couldn't find a way out, couldn't make amends, couldn't find inner peace, unless we all paid with our lives. We all had a responsibility.\" [2459] \"He made you... help him kill Harriet and Ida?\" I asked, trying to clarify the situation. [2460] \"Yes, they, and myself,\" said Jeanne. \"He insisted that we all had to die, otherwise it wouldn't have any meaning. He didn't want to be a murderer, he wanted to be an executor - he used that word several times, which means that neither of us could escape punishment. I agreed that Harriet and Ida should die, they were too evil. But... I didn't want to die, nor did I want Richard to die. I think what he did was sincere repentance for what he had done, and that was enough for Patrick, and for any higher power that might exist or not exist. But I couldn't convince Richard. I soon realized that there was no point in trying to convince him. He was as clever as ever, but he had lost some parts that made him paranoid, strange, and come up with many absurd ideas. He never forgot that event, those sins... He became a strange madman. I knew that if I didn't comply with his proposal, he would kill me. He didn't say it outright because he didn't want me to be coerced. You know, he was very good to me, he just needed an ally, someone who shared his views. He sincerely believed that I would agree to his plot because he thought I was more reasonable than Harriet and Ida. He was too confident in his own opinion, believing that only by sacrificing our four lives could he atone for his sins. I think he may have been right, but I was afraid. But now I'm not afraid anymore, and I don't know what changed me. Maybe, despite living in pain all these years, I still held a glimmer of hope that things would get better. Sadness does not equal despair.\" [2461] \"You knew you had to pretend to comply to save your life,\" said Poirot. \"Perfectly deceiving Richard Nix was the only way to escape death. But you didn't know how, so you turned to Nancy Drew.\" [2462] \"Yes, I went to her. She helped me, or so I thought. Her plan was brilliant. She suggested that I change one part of the plan. Richard's original plan was that once Harriet and Ada were dead, he would kill me and then commit suicide. Naturally, as someone who enjoyed being in control and being the authority, he wanted to be in charge from start to finish. [2463] \"Nancy told me to convince Richard that I should kill him, not the other way around. 'Impossible!' I said, 'He won't agree.' But Nancy said he would, as long as I presented it the right way. She advised me to act more obsessed with our goal than he was. She was right; it worked. I went to Richard and told him that it wasn't enough for just the four of us\u2014me, him, Harriet, and Ada\u2014to die. Nancy had to be punished too. I told him that Nancy was even more evil than Harriet, and how she had plotted to steal Patrick away from his wife and wouldn't stop until she succeeded. I also told him that Nancy had confessed to me that her speech at the King's Head Inn wasn't meant to help Patrick, but to hurt Francis. Nancy wanted Francis to commit suicide or at least give up Patrick and return to Cambridge to be with her father, so she could take his place.\" [2464] \"More lies,\" said Poirot. [2465] \"Yes, more lies\u2014but these were Nancy's ideas, and they worked! Richard agreed that I should die first.\" [2466] \"And he didn't know about Samuel Kid, did he?\" said Poirot. [2467] \"No, he didn't. Nancy and I brought in Sem, who was part of our plan. Neither Nancy nor I wanted to climb out the window, down the tree, and risk breaking our necks after locking the door and hiding the key behind the tiles. But that was the only way out of Room 238, so we needed Samuel's help. He not only did that but also impersonated Richard.\" [2468] \"Hiding the key behind the tiles,\" I murmured to myself, confirming the details in my mind. \"So, the story you told us at Mr. Kid's house was seamless, Richard Naylors hiding the key to make us think the killer took it, so he could frame Nancy Drew for the murder at the Blaux Han hotel.\" [2469] \"That's right,\" said Poirot. \"More precisely, he thought that was the case. When Jenny gave him the poisoned water, he still believed Jenny would live and ensure that Nancy Drew was accused of the murder at the Blaux Han Hotel. He believed Jenny would report to the police and make sure they suspected Nancy. But he didn't know that Nancy had prepared a solid alibi, that she was with the Wallace couple at that time! He also didn't know that a brooch would be placed in his mouth, that the key would be hidden behind the tiles, and that the windows would be open...\"\n[2470] \"Yes, Richard didn't know all of that,\" agreed Jenny. \"You now understand why I said Nancy's plan was brilliant, Mr. Poirot.\" [2471] \"She's a talented artist, madam. A clever artist who can see how to design details and also grasp the framework, knowing how to integrate all the elements together.\" [2472] Jenny turned to me and said, \"Nancy and I didn't want to do this, you have to believe me, Mr. Ketchup. If I had opposed Mr. Richard's plan, he would have killed me.\" She sighed. \"We planned it all, Nancy would escape unharmed, and Nancy and I would be punished for framing and accusing her, but not with the death penalty. We hoped to serve a few months in prison. After that, I planned to marry Sammy.\" [2473] \"Madam, everything was already ruined. I know you killed Harriet Sipper and Richard Nigges.\" [2474] \"I didn't kill Richard, Mr. Poirot. You're wrong about that.\" [2475] \"Yes, but you deceived him. Richard Nigges agreed to die because you agreed to participate in his plan, that all four of you would die. Then you brought Nancy Dunn into it, and the plan became five people. But you didn't really agree to his plan. You betrayed him and set up another trap behind his back. Who knows if Richard Nigges would have chosen to die in that way if he had known about your secret agreement with Nancy Dunn?\" [2476] Jane's expression stiffened. \"I didn't kill Richard Nigg. I killed him in self-defense, or he would have killed me.\" [2477] \"You said he didn't explicitly say he would kill you.\" [2478] \"No, but I knew he would. What do you think, Mr. Cattermole? Did I kill Richard Nigg?\" [2479] \"I don't know.\" [2480] \"Cattermole, my friend, don't be silly.\" [2481] \"He's not silly.\" Jinnie said. \"He's using the part of his brain you don't want to use, Mr. Poirot. Please, just think again, please. I would like to hear you say I didn't murder Richard Nigg before I'm hanged.\" [2482] I stood up and said: \"Let's go, Poirot. I wanted to end this conversation while the echo of 'hope' still lingered in the room.\" [2483] Epilogue\n[2484] Four days later, I sat by the fireplace in Mrs. Blanche Onsworth's house, sipping a glass of brandy and engrossed in a crossword puzzle when Poirot entered the living room and stood silently beside me for several minutes. I didn't look up at him. [2485] Finally, he cleared his throat and said: \"Caderpillar, you still won't talk to me about how Richard Nigg died - whether he was murdered, committed suicide with assistance, or killed in self-defense?\" [2486] \"I don't see the point in arguing about it,\" I said, feeling a spasm in my stomach. I no longer wanted to discuss the murder at the Baskerville Hotel. What I wanted to do, or needed to do, was to write it all down, to put the whole story on paper in detail. Was there such a difference between saying something and writing it down? [2487] \"Don't be afraid, my friend,\" said Poirot. \"I won't mention it again. We can talk about something else, like how I went to the 'Jolly Coffee Shop' this morning. Miss Spence asked me to tell you that she'd like to talk to you when you're free. She's a bit upset.\" [2488] \"With me?\" [2489] \"Yes. She said she was just hearing about the case at the Baskerville Hotel one moment, and the next, it was all over. A murder happened right in front of everyone, and yet no one saw the end. Miss Spence wants you to tell her the whole story from beginning to end.\" [2490] \"It wasn't my fault that murder happened,\" I whispered. \"Why can't she read about it in the newspaper like everyone else?\" [2491] \"No, she wants to talk to you alone. As a waitress, she's quite intelligent and a rare woman. Don't you think so, my friend?\" [2492] \"I know what you're up to, Poirot,\" I said weakly. \"Just drop it, stop wasting time, and tell Phyllida... sorry... you see, I'm busy. Can you leave?\" [2493] \"You're angry with me.\" [2494] \"Yes, a bit,\" I admitted. \"Henry Nayes and the suitcase, Raffles Burbank and the laundry cart, Thomas Brine and his girlfriend in the hotel garden, who was wearing a light brown coat, half of England wears such coats. And the handcart\u2026\"\n[2495] \"Ah!\" [2496] \"Yes, 'Ah'. You know that Jeanne Hobby isn't dead, yet you deliberately mislead me, making me suspect that her body was moved from Room 402, in the most impractical ways?\" [2497] \"My friend, because I wanted to encourage you to use your imagination. If you don't consider the most unlikely possibilities, you can't become the best detective. I'm teaching you to use your grey cells, forcing them to move in a different direction, so that you can be inspired.\" [2498] \"Do as you please.\" [2499] \"You must think that Poirot is going too far, beyond what is necessary. Maybe so.\" [2500] \"In Room 402, you made a fuss over those bloodstains, the direction of the bloodstains, the width of the door... What does it all mean? You know that Jeanne Hobby wasn't killed and wasn't dragged anywhere.\" [2501] \"I know, but you don't. You and our friend Mr. Lazari both believe that Jeanie is dead and that the blood on the floor belongs to her. Oh, I want you to think for yourself: a suitcase, a wheeled laundry cart - these two tools can directly enter Room 402 and be placed next to the body. So why did the killer drag the body towards the door? He couldn't have done that! Therefore, she couldn't have been dead! The bloodstains dragged towards the door were a deception, intended to make us believe that the body was moved elsewhere. This detail was done very convincingly, so it was very important as evidence at the crime scene. [2502] \"But for Hercule Poirot, it was just a small detail that allowed him to verify his suspicions. Jeanie Hobbs was not killed in that room, and no one was killed. Because I can't think of a reason to drag a bloody body to the door. No killer would walk around the hotel's public corridor with a victim's body, unless they put it in a container. But I can think of containers that can easily be brought into the room and directly put the body in and take it away, without dragging the body to the door. Caderpillar, this logic is very simple. I'm surprised you didn't think of it immediately.\" [2503] \"It's simple for you.\" I said, \"Next time, if you want me to think of something immediately, just tell me directly. Whatever it is, be direct, and it will save a lot of trouble.\" [2504] He smiled. \"Okay. I will learn to be more direct from my good friend Caderpillar. I will start learning now!\" [2505] \"If by 'private affairs' you mean Phyllis Spoon, I can tell you that we are impossible, forever impossible.\" I glanced at the letter in his hand. \"What about the private affairs of another pitiful person? Why thank you?\" [2506] Poirot smiled. \"Dr. Ambrose Flavard and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter. [2507] \"Who came to the letter?\u201d\n[2508] Poirot smiled. \"Ambrose Flavard, M.D., and his wife.\u201d He handed me the letter.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."